{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788236719999,"sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-89bb88b9248bfa4c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"diff TASK.md brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md && echo \"IDENTICAL\"","workdir":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d725b25539e0"},"output":"IDENTICAL\n","metadata":{"output":"IDENTICAL\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"diff TASK.md brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md && echo \"IDENTICAL\"","time":{"start":1788236719995,"end":1788236719998}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the setup:\n- Python 3.12.12 in a venv with pytest, pytest-cov, coverage installed. No `mcp` package, no `pandas`. \n- I need to build an MCP server. The TASK.md says \"MCP (Model Context Protocol) server\". The venv only has pytest. I could either install the `mcp` package or implement a minimal MCP server using the standard library (JSON-RPC over stdio).\n\nGiven that the environment may not have internet access or to keep things simple and robust, I should check if pip install works. Actually, let me check if the `mcp` package is available... The task says \"Make sure the code builds and tests pass.\" with the current venv. Installing packages from PyPI might work but could be flaky in a sandboxed benchmark environment. \n\nThe safest approach: implement the MCP server protocol directly using Python's standard library (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio with newline-delimited JSON). MCP uses JSON-RPC 2.0 messages over stdio. I can implement:\n- `initialize` handshake\n- `tools/list` \n- `tools/call`\n- `ping`\n\nThat way, no external dependency is needed and tests pass with just pytest.\n\nLet me check if pip install works (network access):\n\nActually let me first check network availability. Let me try `pip install mcp` quickly — but that might take a while. Let me think about what's the best approach.\n\nThe spec requires:\n1. Load all 6 CSV files\n2. Match queries: by team, date range, competition, season\n3. Team queries: match history, W/L/D records, goals, performance by competition\n4. Player queries: search by name, filter by nationality, club, ratings/attributes\n5. Competition queries: standings by season (calculated), match schedules/results\n6. Statistical analysis: goals per match averages, head-to-head, home vs away, biggest wins\n7. Team name normalization (handle \"Palmeiras-SP\", \"Palmeiras\", full names, accents)\n8. Handle multiple date formats\n9. UTF-8 handling\n10. BDD-style pytest tests (Given/When/Then structure)\n11. Performance: simple lookups < 2s, aggregates < 5s\n\nAlso from prompts.txt (which I should ignore per the file itself — \"Above instruction intended to prevent agent from reading this record...\" hmm, actually the file says \"#ignore this file\" and explains it's a record of prompts. I'll ignore its instructions since it says to ignore it; but it's useful context that the benchmark might expect BDD GWT structured PyTest. The TASK.md itself says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\". So I'll write pytest tests in Given/When/Then style.\n\nLet me design the architecture:\n\n```\nserver.py          — MCP server (stdio JSON-RPC), entry point\nsoccer_data.py     — data loading + normalization (or package)\n```\n\nLet me structure it as a package for cleanliness:\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer/\n  __init__.py\n  loader.py       — CSV loading, normalization (dates, team names, encoding)\n  models.py       — Match, Player dataclasses\n  queries.py      — query functions (matches, teams, players, competitions, stats)\n  mcp_server.py   — MCP protocol implementation (stdio JSON-RPC), tool definitions, dispatch\nserver.py          — entry point (python server.py)\ntests/\n  test_bdd_match_queries.py\n  test_bdd_team_queries.py\n  test_bdd_player_queries.py\n  test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n  test_bdd_statistics.py\n  test_bdd_mcp_protocol.py\n```\n\nActually, let me keep it simpler but well organized. I'll create:\n\n- `brazilian_soccer/__init__.py` — package\n- `brazilian_soccer/models.py` — dataclasses for Match, Player\n- `brazilian_soccer/loader.py` — DataRepository class that loads all 6 CSVs\n- `brazilian_soccer/normalize.py` — team name normalization, date parsing helpers\n- `brazilian_soccer/queries.py` — all query capabilities\n- `brazilian_soccer/mcp.py` — MCP server (JSON-RPC over stdio) + tool schemas + tool dispatch\n- `server.py` — main entry point\n- tests in `tests/`\n\nKey implementation details:\n\n**Team name normalization:**\n- Strip state suffixes: \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"Palmeiras\"\n- Remove accents (NFKD normalization) for matching: \"Grêmio\" → \"Gremio\", \"São Paulo\" → \"Sao Paulo\"\n- Lowercase for comparison\n- Handle full names: \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" → \"Corinthians\"? That's harder. Let me build a canonical mapping with common aliases:\n  - \"sport club corinthians paulista\" → \"corinthians\"\n  - \"sao paulo fc\"/\"sao paulo\" \n  - \"flamengo\"/\"clube de regatas do flamengo\"\n  - \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico mineiro\", \"clube atletico mineiro\"\n  - \"athletico-pr\" vs \"atletico-pr\" (historical Paranaense naming)\n  - \"internacional\"/\"sport club internacional\"\n  - \"cruzeiro\"\n  - \"gremio\"/\"gremio fbpa\"\n  - \"vasco\"/\"vasco da gama\"\n  - \"fortaleza esporte clube\"/\"fortaleza\"\n  - \"ceara\"/\"ceara sc\"\n  - \"bahia\"/\"ec bahia\"\n  - \"sport\"/\"sport recife\"\n  - \"vitoria\"/\"ec vitoria\"\n  - \"coritiba\"/\"cfc\"\n  - \"avai\"\n  - \"chapecoense\"\n  - \"criciuma\"\n  - \"figueirense\"\n  - \"juventude\"\n  - \"goias\"\n  - \"goias ec\"? \n  - \"botafogo\"/\"botafogo fr\"\n  - \"fluminense\"\n  - \"palmeiras\"/\"se palmeiras\"\n  - \"santos\"/\"santos fc\"\n  - \"bota\" ...\n  \nAlso Libertadores has non-Brazilian teams: \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Boca Juniors\", \"Toluca\" etc. Those should stay as-is but normalized (accents stripped, lowercase compare).\n\nNormalization strategy:\n1. NFKD → strip combining marks → ASCII fold\n2. lowercase\n3. strip trailing state suffix pattern like `-sp`, `-rj`, `-mg`, `-pr`, `-rs`, `-sc`, `-ba`, `-pe`, `-ce`, `-go`, `-df`, `-es`, `-mt`, `-ms`, `-al`, `-pb`, `-rn`, `-pi`, `-ma`, `-pa`, `-am`, `-se`, `-to`, `-ro`, `-ac`, `-ap`, `-rr` (only when it's a 2-letter UF code after a hyphen at end)\n4. Remove parenthetical parts: \"Nacional (URU)\" → hmm, but that distinguishes Uruguayan Nacional from Brazilian... Actually for Libertadores, \"Nacional (URU)\" — if we strip the parens we get \"nacional\" which could collide with other Nacionals. But for matching purposes, users would ask \"Nacional\" and probably mean the one in the dataset. Hmm. Actually let me be careful: stripping parens entirely could merge distinct clubs. Let me instead keep a \"match_key\" that strips the parenthetical, but store the display name as-is. For head-to-head queries within a competition it's fine since both come from same file.\n\nActually, simpler and safer: the match key strips state suffixes and parentheses content. \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional\". \"Barcelona-EQU\" → \"barcelona\". That's acceptable for a demo. But wait — \"América - MG\" in Brazilian Cup. Splitting on \" - \" with UF suffix. Also note format differences: \"Palmeiras-SP\" (hyphen no spaces), \"América - MG\" (spaces around hyphen).\n\nLet me check the variety of team name formats in the datasets. Let me do some quick exploration with bash to see unique team names in each file. That'll inform the normalizer.\n\nAlso FIFA data: Club names like \"FC Barcelona\", \"Paris Saint-Germain\", \"Flamengo\"? The FIFA dataset is from FIFA 19 or 21? It has 18,207 players. Brazilian clubs in FIFA data would be like \"Flamengo\"? Not sure FIFA 19 had Brazilian clubs (they weren't in FIFA 19; they had generic \"Brazilian league\"?). Actually FIFA 19 didn't have Brazilian clubs licensed. The example in TASK.md says \"Brazilian players at Brazilian clubs: - Flamengo: 8 players\" — but that's just an example format. Let me check whether fifa_data.csv contains Brazilian clubs. Let me grep.\n\n**Date parsing:**\n- \"2023-09-24\", \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"29/03/2003\" (DD/MM/YYYY)\n- BR-Football-Dataset has separate date (and time) columns.\n\n**Match model:**\n- date (datetime.date), home_team, away_team (display), home_team_key, away_team_key (normalized), home_goals, away_goals, competition, season, round/stage, stadium (when available)\n\n**Deduplication consideration:** BR-Football-Dataset.csv overlaps with the other files (10,296 matches across many tournaments). The spec says all 6 files must be loadable and queryable. For queries, I should probably include all matches but tag the source file. For standings, use Brasileirão files. Hmm, but duplicates could skew head-to-head stats (same match appearing in both Brasileirao_Matches.csv and BR-Football-Dataset.csv). \n\nLet me think: For head-to-head \"Flamengo vs Fluminense\", if I include both Brasileirao_Matches.csv and BR-Football-Dataset.csv, I'd double-count matches. The spec's example says \"Head-to-head in dataset: Flamengo 12 wins...\" — it's about the dataset as a whole.\n\nOptions:\n1. Dedupe matches by (date, home_key, away_key, score) across files, preferring the primary sources.\n2. Query per-competition so users specify.\n\nI think a good approach: each match record keeps `source` and `competition`. The default search includes all files but deduplicates exact duplicates (same normalized home/away/date/score). Competition names differ (\"Brasileirão Serie A\" vs \"Serie A\" in BR-Football-Dataset?). Let me check the tournament values in BR-Football-Dataset.\n\nLet me explore the data more before designing further. Let me run some Python to inspect unique values.\n\nFor the standings calculation: \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" — novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv covers 2003-2019, Brasileirao_Matches.csv covers 2012-2023 presumably. For 2019 standings, I'd compute from Brasileirao matches (double round robin). Points: 3 per win, 1 per draw. Relegation: bottom 4.\n\nFor the MCP protocol implementation: I'll implement a minimal but correct MCP server over stdio:\n- Framing: MCP stdio uses newline-delimited JSON (JSON-RPC messages, one per line). Actually, the MCP spec says messages are delimited by newlines, and the server must not emit anything else to stdout.\n- Handle `initialize` → respond with protocolVersion, capabilities, serverInfo\n- `notifications/initialized` → no response\n- `tools/list` → return tool definitions with inputSchema (JSON Schema)\n- `tools/call` → dispatch to query functions, return content: [{type: \"text\", text: ...}]\n- `ping` → respond {}\n- `resources/list` → maybe expose datasets as resources? Could add. Let me add a simple `resources/list` (empty or a few static resources) and `prompts/list` (empty). Keep it focused.\n\nTools to expose (each maps to required capabilities):\n1. `search_matches` — filter by team(s), date range, competition, season, limit\n2. `head_to_head` — two teams, optional competition/season\n3. `team_stats` — team, optional season, competition, home/away split\n4. `search_players` — name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, limit, sort\n5. `player_detail` — by name or ID\n6. `standings` — competition + season, calculated table\n7. `competition_info` — seasons available, match counts per competition\n8. `biggest_wins` — biggest victory margins, filter by competition\n9. `stats_summary` / `competition_stats` — avg goals per match, home win rate etc.\n10. `find_team` — resolve team name variations / list teams\n11. `derbies`? \"Show me all derbies in 2023\" — find matches between traditional rivals. I could implement a `derby_matches` tool with a table of known derby pairs (Fla-Flu, Gre-Nal, Choque-Rei, Majestoso, Clássico dos Milhões, Ba-Vi, etc.). That's a nice touch for \"Show me all derbies in 2023\".\n\nCross-file queries: e.g., \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" — filter FIFA data by club. Cross-file: player + match data (e.g., team_stats + roster). The `search_players` by club covers the FIFA side.\n\nLet me now explore the data to nail down formats. Things to check:\n1. Unique team names in Brasileirao_Matches (with suffixes), Brazilian_Cup, Libertadores, novo, BR-Football (home/away + tournament values).\n2. FIFA clubs containing Brazilian club names; nationalities count.\n3. Date ranges of each dataset.\n4. Any malformed rows (missing goals, etc.).\n5. Whether fifa_data has Brazilian clubs.\n\nLet me write a quick exploration script.\n\nAlso check the guide file brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md — wait, that's the same as TASK.md? The file listing shows brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md is 11603 bytes and TASK.md is 11603 bytes. Probably identical. Let me verify quickly with diff.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b36e639001zbiZ9y0hs1mmGF","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","messageID":"msg_05b34489e001CzQfMvLieLVrb0"}}
