Atlético Tigre': 28\n  'Clube Sport Marítimo': 23\n  'Cruzeiro': 20\n  'Fluminense': 20\n  'Grêmio': 20\n  'Internacional': 20\n  'Paraná': 20\n  'Real Sporting de Gijón': 30\n  'Santos': 20\n  'Santos Laguna': 26\n  'Sport Club do Recife': 20\n  'Sporting CP': 30\n  'Sporting Kansas City': 27\n  'Sporting Lokeren': 28\n  'Sporting de Charleroi': 28\n  'VfL Sportfreunde Lotte': 28\n  'Vitória': 20\n  'Vitória Guimarães': 30\n  'Vitória de Setúbal': 28\n\nnovo all teams: ['América-MG', 'América-RN', 'Athletico-PR', 'Atlético-GO', 'Atlético-MG', 'Avaí', 'Bahia', 'Barueri', 'Botafogo-RJ', 'Brasiliense', 'CSA', 'Ceará', 'Chapecoense', 'Corinthians', 'Coritiba', 'Criciúma', 'Cruzeiro', 'Figueirense', 'Flamengo', 'Fluminense', 'Fortaleza', 'Goiás', 'Grêmio', 'Grêmio Prudente', 'Guarani', 'Internacional', 'Ipatinga', 'Joinville', 'Juventude', 'Náutico', 'Palmeiras', 'Paraná', 'Paysandu', 'Ponte Preta', 'Portuguesa', 'Santa Cruz', 'Santo André', 'Santos', 'Sport', 'São Caetano', 'São Paulo', 'Vasco', 'Vitória']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\n\ndef quick(path):\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nrows = quick('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\nfor t in ['Serie A','Serie B','Serie C','Copa do Brasil']:\n    ss = sorted({r['date'][:4] for r in rows if r['tournament']==t})\n    print(t, \"seasons:\", ss)\n# sample ht/at result values\nprint(\"ht_result values:\", collections.Counter(r['ht_result'] for r in rows[:2000]))\n\nrows2 = quick('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv')\nbyseason = collections.defaultdict(set)\nfor r in rows2:\n    byseason[r['season']].add(int(r['round']))\nfor s in sorted(byseason, key=int):\n    print(\"Cup\", s, \"rounds:\", sorted(byseason[s]))\n\n# FIFA brazilian clubs (match against known names)\nrows3 = quick('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv')\nclubs = collections.Counter(r['Club'] for r in rows3)\nknown = ['Flamengo','Palmeiras','Corinthians','São Paulo','Sao Paulo','Grêmio','Gremio','Fluminense','Botafogo','Internacional','Cruzeiro','Vasco','Bahia','Fortaleza','Ceará','Ceara','Sport','Goiás','Goias','Athletico','Atlético','Atletico','Santos','Chapecoense','Paraná','Parana','Avai','Avaí','Vitória','Vitoria','Coritiba','Criciúma','Criciuma','América','America']\nhits = {c: n for c, n in clubs.items() if any(k.lower() in c.lower() for k in known)}\nprint(\"\\nFIFA clubs matching Brazilian names:\")\nfor c, n in sorted(hits.items()):\n    print(f\"  {c!r}: {n}\")\n\n# novo full team list\nrows4 = quick('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nteams = set()\nfor r in rows4:\n    teams.add(r['Equipe_mandante']); teams.add(r['Equipe_visitante'])\nprint(\"\\nnovo all teams:\", sorted(teams))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788236926463,"end":1788236926482}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Excellent. Now I understand the data well:\n\n1. **Brasileirao_Matches.csv**: 4180 rows, seasons 2012-2022, teams with `-UF` suffixes. Goals are ints.\n2. **Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv**: 1337 rows, seasons 2012-2021, rounds 1-8 (numbers). Team names like \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" and \"América - MG\".\n3. **Libertadores_Matches.csv**: 1255 rows, seasons 2013-2022, stages (group stage, round of 16, quarterfinals, semifinals, final). One \"NA\" row with \"-\" goals (a final that was presumably not played in dataset). Teams include international ones with country qualifiers like \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Olimpia-PAR\", \"Atlético-MG\".\n4. **BR-Football-Dataset.csv**: 10296 rows, tournaments: Serie A (3689), Serie B (3677), Serie C (1807), Copa do Brasil (1123). Goals are floats (\"1.0\"). Team names vary: \"Sao Paulo\" (no accents!), \"America MG\", \"America RN\", \"ASA AL\", \"Aguia Negra-MS\" — so accents are already stripped in this file. Dates \"2023-09-24\" plus separate time column.\n5. **novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv**: 6886 rows, 2003-2019, Portuguese columns, dates DD/MM/YYYY, has Arena (stadium), Vencedor. 43 teams. Names like \"Athletico-PR\", \"Atlético-GO\", \"Grêmio Prudente\", \"América-MG\".\n6. **fifa_data.csv**: 18207 rows, 827 Brazilians. Brazilian clubs present: Grêmio, Atlético Mineiro, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Internacional, Botafogo, Bahia, Atlético Paranaense, Sport Club do Recife, Ceará Sporting Club, Flamengo? Let me check for Flamengo/Palmeiras/Corinthians/São Paulo/Vasco — the grep didn't include all. \"Flamengo\" was in the list of keys... it wasn't in output, hmm, output was truncated to 30. Let me check specifically. Actually the printed list was `brz[:30]` and Flamengo wasn't in the first 30 alphabetically... it's not sorted by name necessarily (Counter order). Let me check directly for key clubs later.\n\nNote FIFA has no \"São Paulo FC\"? Ceará Sporting Club exists. Let me check quickly for the main Brazilian clubs in FIFA data.\n\nKey data quality notes:\n- Libertadores \"final\" stage has 15 rows (finals are two-legged except some).\n- The NA row: Flamengo vs Athletico final (2022 Libertadores final was Flamengo vs Athletico-PR... actually that was 2022. The row has no data — skip rows with missing/NA goals or datetime).\n- BR-Football goals are floats.\n- novo has accented names; BR-Football has unaccented names.\n\n**Team normalization plan:**\n- `normalize_team(name)`:\n  - strip whitespace\n  - remove parenthetical suffixes like \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\"? Hmm, that's a long historical note. \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" — the state suffix is \"- RJ\" at the end. The parenthetical is part of club identity. For match key: remove parentheses content, remove trailing state suffix, strip accents, lowercase, collapse spaces/hyphens.\n  - \"Nacional (URU)\" → strip \"(URU)\" → \"nacional\". Hmm, but then Nacional (PAR) also → \"nacional\". Both are distinct clubs (Uruguayan and Paraguayan). If I strip country qualifiers, \"Nacional\" matches both — for a demo that's acceptable; the user asking for \"Nacional\" matches gets both. But head-to-head between \"Nacional (URU)\" and someone is fine. Actually wait — could two different teams with the same normalized key play each other? Like \"Nacional (URU)\" vs \"Nacional (PAR)\"? That would be weird but possible in Libertadores. Let me not overthink: keep a `match_key` = accent-stripped, lowercase, state-suffix-removed, parens-content-removed name. Also keep display name.\n  \n  Actually, I should be more careful about the state suffix removal: \"América - MG\" → \"america mg\" → remove \" mg\" suffix. \"Aguia Negra-MS\" → \"aguia negra\". \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras\". \"Atletico-GO\" → \"atletico go\"?? No wait — \"Atletico-GO\" the \"-GO\" is a state suffix → \"atletico\". But \"Atlético-GO\" and \"Atlético-MG\" both start \"Atlético\". After normalization: \"atletico go\" → strip trailing UF token \"go\" → \"atletico\"; \"atletico mg\" → \"atletico\". Good, distinct.\n  \n  But careful: teams like \"Santos FC\" vs \"Santos\" — collapse \"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"acb\"? Hmm. In BR-Football: \"ABC\", \"ASA\", \"ASA AL\", \"America MG\", \"America RN\", \"Americano RJ\". In FIFA: \"Ceará Sporting Club\" vs BR-Football \"Ceara\"? The FIFA clubs are separate entities from match teams (they're player clubs). Cross-file queries: \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" — filter FIFA by club normalized. So the FIFA club normalizer should map \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → \"ceara\" and match \"Ceara\" → \"ceara\"? For cross-file matching I need both to normalize to the same key.\n  \n  Plan for canonical matching: \n  1. Unicode NFKD, strip combining marks (so \"Grêmio\"→\"Gremio\", \"São\"→\"Sao\")\n  2. lowercase\n  3. replace common legal-form tokens: \"sport club\", \"clube de regatas\", \"esporte clube\", \"futebol clube\"? These appear inside names though. Simpler: remove standalone tokens from a stopword set: {\"fc\",\"ec\",\"sc\",\"acb\",\"spfc\",\"fbc\"?...}. Hmm \"São Paulo FC\" → \"sao paulo\" after removing \"fc\" token. \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" → removing \"sport club\" would give \"corinthians paulista\" — not \"corinthians\". So I need an explicit alias table for full official names:\n  \n  ```\n  \"sport club corinthians paulista\" -> corinthians\n  \"clube de regatas do flamengo\" -> flamengo\n  \"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\" -> palmeiras\n  \"clube atletico mineiro\" -> atletico mg?? \n  ```\n  \n  Hmm wait. \"Atletico-MG\" normalizes (strip -MG) to \"atletico\". \"Clube Atlético Mineiro\" normalizes to \"clube atletico mineiro\". Alias table maps that to \"atletico mg\"... but our key for the team from Brasileirao is \"atletico\" after stripping the state. That's a problem: \"Atlético-MG\" → \"atletico\" and \"Atlético-GO\" → \"atletico\" COLLIDE! \n\n  Oh no. \"Atletico-GO\" (Atlético Goianiense) and \"Atletico-MG\" (Atlético Mineiro) are different clubs! If I strip state suffixes blindly, they collide. Also \"America-MG\" vs \"America-RN\" → both \"america\"! And \"América-SC\"? Hmm.\n\n  So blindly stripping state suffixes is WRONG for distinctness. Better approach: strip the suffix form for *matching* but keep disambiguation... Let me think.\n\n  The spec says: 'Team Name Variations: \"Palmeiras-SP\", \"Palmeiras\", \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" — Implementation should normalize team names for consistent matching.'\n\n  The right approach: canonical key should DROP the state suffix only when it doesn't disambiguate. Actually, the standard approach for this Brazilian data:\n  - \"Palmeiras-SP\" and \"Palmeiras\" → same club \"palmeiras\"\n  - \"Atletico-MG\" (Atlético Mineiro, from Belo Horizonte) — its common name IS \"Atlético-MG\" or \"Atlético\"; \"Atlético-GO\" (Goianiense) common name \"Atlético-GO\".\n  - \"America-MG\" vs \"America-RN\" vs \"America-SC\": distinct clubs.\n  \n  So canonical key = name WITHOUT state suffix can collide. Instead: canonical key = name with state suffix where present, and alias map for plain names:\n  - \"palmeiras sp\" == \"palmeiras\" == \"se palmeiras\"\n  - \"atletico mg\" == \"atletico mineiro\" == \"clube atletico mineiro\" == \"atlético\" (common usage for MG)... but plain \"atletico\" alone is ambiguous.\n  \n  Hmm, but what does the user ask? \"What is Atletico-MG's record?\" or \"Which team scored most goals?\" — team list queries use the dataset names.\n\n  Pragmatic approach used by many implementations:\n  - Keep `state_suffix` separately when available (the Brasileirao file has home_team_state columns!).\n  - Canonical name = base name (state-stripped, accent-stripped for matching) + we keep a map base_name → set of full variants.\n  \n  For collisions like \"america\" → {America-MG, America-RN, America-SC}: when a user searches \"america\", return all variants. When user searches \"america-mg\", return just that. When computing standings, I group by the exact dataset team name (normalized WITH suffix), which keeps clubs distinct.\n\n  So two-level design:\n  - `match_key(team_str)` = normalized full name with suffix: lowercase, accents stripped, punctuation collapsed, state suffix preserved: \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras sp\"?? Hmm wait, but then \"Palmeiras\" (from Libertadores file, no suffix) → \"palmeiras\" ≠ \"palmeiras sp\". They wouldn't join across files for head-to-head.\n\n  Ugh. Brasileirao: \"Palmeiras-SP\". Libertadores: \"Atlético-MG\" (with suffix), also \"Flamengo\" (no suffix). Cup: \"América - MG\". BR-Football: \"Sao Paulo\" (no suffix, no accents). novo: \"Grêmio\" (no suffix, accents), \"América-MG\" (suffix).\n\n  The suffix presence is inconsistent across files for the same club. E.g., Flamengo appears as \"Flamengo-RJ\" (Brasileirao), \"Flamengo\" (Libertadores), \"Flamengo\" (BR-Football), \"Flamengo\" (novo). If key = \"flamengo rj\" vs \"flamengo\", head-to-head across competitions would split into two entities. BAD.\n\n  So I DO want to strip state suffixes to join across files — that's the whole point of normalization. But handle collisions:\n  - \"america\" collides (MG, RN, SC). \n  - \"atletico\" collides (MG, GO, PR?).\n  - \"internacional\" no collision. \"nacional\" collides (URU, PAR).\n  - \"sport\" (Recife) vs \"Sport\" ... only one Sport in Brazilian files. \n  - \"vitoria\" (BA) vs \"Vitoria\" (ES)! Both exist: \"Vitória-BA\" and \"Vitória-ES\"? In Serie B data probably \"Vitoria BA\" and \"Vitoria ES\"? Hmm BR-Football: does it have \"Vitoria BA\"? Let me check. \n\n  Resolution strategy: canonical key strips the suffix, BUT I maintain a collision registry: base names that map to multiple distinct clubs get disambiguated. For queries, when a user says \"atletico mg\", I match by full variant. When user says ambiguous \"atletico\", I can return all clubs matching / or the best fuzzy.\n\n  Practical matching approach used in real implementations of this Kaggle data:\n  - normalize(name) strips accents, lowercases, removes trailing UF suffix (2-letter state code after hyphen/space), removes legal-form tokens (fc, ec, ...), collapses whitespace.\n  - Build entity per (base_key) but store all variants. For collisions, entity includes multiple \"clubs\" and we disambiguate via the state when the user provides it: if the user query includes a state suffix (\"atletico-mg\"), we match entities whose variants include that suffixed form; otherwise match by base name and possibly return multiple.\n\n  For STATS (standings, team records, head-to-head), the unit should be the club entity. If \"america\" maps to America-MG, America-RN, America-SC as separate entities, then stats per entity must use the SUFFIXED name within each file... but then \"Flamengo\" in Libertadores (no suffix) vs \"Flamengo-RJ\" in Brasileirao — same entity, stats should join. \n\n  So entity resolution: base_key + optional state disambiguation. Concretely:\n  1. For each raw team string, compute (base, state) where state = detected trailing UF code (if any).\n  2. Entity id = base if base uniquely maps to one state across the dataset; if multiple states exist for the same base, entity id = base + \" \" + state for each, and matches without state are ambiguous.\n  \n  Wait, but that breaks \"Flamengo-RJ\" (Brasileirao, state RJ) + \"Flamengo\" (Libertadores, no state). Entity \"flamengo\" states={RJ, None} → unique → id \"flamengo\". Good.\n  \"America\" states={MG, RN, SC, ...} → ambiguous → entities \"america mg\", \"america rn\", \"america sc\"... and matches for state-less \"america\" return all three (or the tool reports ambiguity). Matches for \"america-mg\" → \"america mg\". \n\n  But wait: what about \"Atletico\" states={MG, GO, PR (as \"Athletico-PR\"? no that's \"athletico\")}. \"Atlético-PR\" is spelled \"Athletico-PR\" in newer seasons and \"Atlético-PR\" in older. Same club! Accent-stripping: \"atletico-pr\" vs \"athletico-pr\" → bases \"atletico\" vs \"athletico\" — DIFFERENT strings. Need alias: \"athletico\" ≡ \"atletico\". Similarly \"Paraná\"? The club is commonly \"Athletico-PR\" since 2019 rebrand. I'll add alias mapping.\n\n  Also \"Cuiaba\" vs \"Cuiabá\" — accent strip handles. \"Avai\"/\"Avaí\" — handled.\n\n  Known aliases needed (accent-free, lowercase, suffix-stripped):\n  - \"athletico\" ↔ \"atletico\" (only in PR context? \"Athletico-PR\" → base \"athletico\" state PR; \"Atlético-PR\" → base \"atletico\" state PR; map \"athletico\"+\"pr\" → \"atletico pr\" entity.) Hmm, but \"Atlético-MG\" → \"atletico mg\". So base collision of atletico/athletico with states MG/GO/PR: after unifying athletico→atletico, states = {mg, go, pr} → three entities: atletico mg (mineiro), atletico go (goianiense), atletico pr (paranaense). And user query \"atletico-pr\" → entity \"atletico pr\". Query \"athletico\" → base \"athletico\"→alias→\"atletico\", state none → ambiguous (3 clubs) → return all or pick best. OK, acceptable: I'll return the ambiguous set with a note, or let fuzzy pick. For the derby tools and examples in spec, queries like \"Palmeiras\", \"Flamengo\", \"Corinthians\", \"Santos\" are unambiguous.\n  - \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico mg\"? FIFA club \"Atlético Mineiro\" → base \"atletico mineiro\" (no state) → alias to entity \"atletico mg\". Similarly \"Atlético Paranaense\" (FIFA) → \"atletico pr\"; \"Atlético Goianiense\"? FIFA might have \"Atlético GO\"? Let me check FIFA Brazilian clubs more carefully.\n  - \"botafogo fr\"/\"botafogo rj\"/\"botafogo\" \n  - \"gremio fbpa\"? not in data probably.\n  - \"sao paulo fc\"/\"sao paulo\"\n  - \"ceara sporting club\" → \"ceara\"\n  - \"sport club do recife\" → \"sport recife\"? In FIFA: \"Sport Club do Recife\". In BR-Football/novo: \"Sport\" or \"Sport-PE\"? novo sample had... let me check \"Sport\" in novo. I saw teams list only first 25. Probably \"Sport\" or \"Sport Recife\". Alias both → \"sport recife\"? Hmm, but Brasileirao file has \"Sport-PE\" → base \"sport\", state PE → entity \"sport pe\"... ugh. If entity = \"sport pe\" but FIFA club = \"Sport Club do Recife\" → alias → \"sport recife\" ≠ \"sport pe\". Need alias \"sport club do recife\" → \"sport\" with state pe... \n\n  This is getting complex. Let me simplify with a well-designed alias table where I map common full names to the canonical suffixed base used in the match files, and make the state handling part of the canonical id.\n\n  Cleaner design:\n  \n  **canonical_team(name) → (base, state)**:\n  1. strip accents, lowercase, trim\n  2. remove parenthetical content: \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional\", \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → \"boavista sport club - rj\"\n  3. apply alias replacements for full official names (substring/whole match): \n     - \"sport club corinthians paulista\" → \"corinthians\"\n     - \"clube de regatas do flamengo\" → \"flamengo\"\n     - \"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\"\n     - \"clube atletico mineiro\" / \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico mg\" (treat \"mineiro\" as state hint mg)\n     - \"atletico paranaense\" / \"athletico paranaense\" → \"atletico pr\" (hmm \"paranaense\" → pr)\n     - \"atletico goianiense\" → \"atletico go\"\n     - \"sport club do recife\" / \"sport recife\" → \"sport pe\"\n     - \"ceara sporting club\" → \"ceara ce\"? Hmm, BR-Football \"Ceara\"? and novo \"Ceará\". Suffix-less. So canonical for Ceará = \"ceara\". FIFA \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → alias → \"ceara\". But wait if I map \"ceara sporting club\" → \"ceara ce\" that breaks. Just map to \"ceara\".\n     - \"vitoria ba\" vs \"vitoria es\": FIFA \"Esporte Clube Vitória\" → \"vitoria ba\"? EC Vitória is Bahia. Rio Branco/ES \"Vitória-ES\" hmm.\n  4. detect trailing state: regex `[- ](ac|al|ap|am|ba|ce|df|es|go|ma|mt|ms|mg|pa|pb|pr|pe|pi|rj|rn|rs|ro|rr|sc|sp|se|to)$` → strip it, remember state.\n  5. token cleanup: remove standalone legal tokens {\"fc\",\"ec\",\"sc\"?...}. Careful: \"SC\" as \"Santa Catarina\" suffix? \"Chapecoense-SC\" — the suffix -SC is Santa Catarina state, handled by step 4. Standalone token \"sc\" in \"Ceará Sporting Club\"? I handle that via explicit alias above. Hmm, generic removal of \"sc\" token might break something like... \"SC Internacional\"? Not in data. Let me not do generic legal-token removal; rely on explicit aliases for the FIFA full names. Actually BR-Football has names like \"America FC Natal\" (RN club full name!). → base \"america fc natal\". Alias: \"america fc natal\" → \"america rn\"? Hmm. Or normalize \"fc\" tokens out: \"america natal\" still ≠ \"america rn\". Ugh.\n\n  OK, I need to stop over-engineering. Let me look at the actual data: what team name variants exist for the major clubs across files, and build a targeted alias table for those. The spec's success criteria: \"Handles team name variations correctly\" — demonstrated with the major clubs. Perfect matching of every tiny Serie C club across files is beyond scope, but I should do reasonably well.\n\n  Let me define:\n  - `team_k