sion, capabilities, clientInfo}. Server responds {protocolVersion, capabilities, serverInfo}.\n  - Client sends `notifications/initialized` (no response).\n  - `tools/list` → {tools: [{name, description, inputSchema}]}\n  - `tools/call` {name, arguments} → {content: [{type:\"text\", text}], isError?}\n  - `ping` → {} \n  - `resources/list` → {resources: []} (or expose the 6 datasets as resources! nice touch: resource URIs like \"soccer://datasets/brasileirao\" with name/description/mimeType text/csv). Let me add resources: 6 dataset resources + read via `resources/read` returning text. That's a nice-to-have; moderate effort. I'll add them — resources/list and resources/read with URIs.\n  - `prompts/list` → {prompts: []}.\n  - Handle unknown method → JSON-RPC error -32601.\n  - Respond to notifications with nothing.\n  - Also `logging`? skip.\n  \n  protocolVersion: echo client's if supported else \"2024-11-05\". I'll accept any and reply with the client's requested version if it's a known one, else \"2024-11-05\".\n\n  Should I use the official `mcp` Python SDK instead? The venv doesn't have it. Try `pip install mcp`? It needs network. Let me test network availability quickly. If available, installing `mcp` (FastMCP) would give a battle-tested server. But the task says \"Make sure the code builds and tests pass\" — a stdlib-only implementation has zero dependency risk in this sandbox. I'll go stdlib-only. That's fully deterministic. Also matches \"framework: unknown, tooling: none\" in stack.json — no external tooling assumed.\n\n  Wait — but should I check if pip install works anyway for a dev dependency? No — stdlib-only keeps it clean. pytest is already available for tests.\n\n  **Entry point**: `server.py` at root: `python server.py` runs the MCP stdio server. Also expose `python -m brazilian_soccer` maybe. And a small CLI: `python server.py --selftest`? I'll add `--query` helpers? Keep focused: server.py runs MCP loop; `cli.py`? Not needed. Actually a tiny CLI is handy for the benchmark harness to verify tools work without speaking MCP: I'll add `python server.py --demo` no... Let me add a `tools_call` test through the protocol in tests instead. Plus `scripts/ask.py`? Hmm, maybe minimal: `server.py` supports `--invoke \"tool_name\" '{\"json\":...}'` for ad-hoc testing. Low cost, useful for the harness. I'll add `--invoke` and `--list-tools` flags.\n\n  **BDD tests**: The spec asks for BDD GWT structured pytest. I'll write tests with Given/When/Then structure (using docstrings or pytest-bdd style without the plugin — no pytest-bdd installed; write GWT-structured test functions with comments/sections: \"Given ... / When ... / Then ...\"). I'll organize:\n\n  - tests/conftest.py — fixtures: repo (session-scoped, loads data), mcp server process helper.\n  - tests/test_match_queries.py — BDD scenarios: matches between two teams; by season; by competition; by date range; last match between two teams.\n  - tests/test_team_queries.py — team stats (Corinthians home record 2022), best away record, goals.\n  - tests/test_player_queries.py — Brazilian players, top-rated at a club, forwards from a club, search by name (Gabriel Barbosa).\n  - tests/test_competition_queries.py — standings 2019 (Flamengo champion), 2018 (Palmeiras), relegation 2019 (none of the relegated set?), competition seasons, Copa do Brasil finals (Libertadores finals).\n  - tests/test_statistics.py — avg goals per match, home win rate, biggest wins, head-to-head.\n  - tests/test_normalization.py — team name variants map to same entity (Palmeiras-SP/Palmeiras/palmeiras; Athletico-PR vs Atlético-PR), date parsing formats, UTF-8 names (Grêmio, Avaí, Fortaleza).\n  - tests/test_mcp_server.py — protocol: initialize, tools/list, tools/call e2e over stdio (subprocess), ping, error on unknown tool, resources.\n  - tests/test_performance.py — simple lookup < 2s, aggregate < 5s.\n\n  Known-fact assertions (from real history, to validate correctness):\n  - 2019 Brasileirão: champion Flamengo. Flamengo points: 90 (I recall 2019: Flamengo 90, Santos 74? Actually Santos 74? Let me recall: 2019 final table: 1 Flamengo 90, 2 Santos 74, 3 Palmeiras 74 — yes matches spec example!). Relegated 2019: CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fortaleza? Hmm 2019 relegated: CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fortaleza? No wait — 2019 relegated were CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fortaleza... I think it was CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fortaleza. Hmm, actually I'm fairly sure: 2019 relegation: CSA (24 pts?), Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fortaleza. Let me not assert exact points; assert champion and maybe relegated set loosely. Let me verify from data itself when writing tests — assert champion names only for safety: 2003 Cruzeiro? Hmm wait, 2003 champion was Cruzeiro (yes, first \"points-based\" champion with run season). 2004: Santos. 2005: Corinthians (after the whistle scandal). 2012: Fluminense. 2013: Cruzeiro. 2014: Cruzeiro. 2015: Corinthians. 2016: Palmeiras. 2017: Corinthians. 2018: Palmeiras. 2019: Flamengo. 2020: Flamengo. 2021: Atlético-MG. 2022: Palmeiras. These are solid facts I'm confident about. I'll assert a handful: 2015 Corinthians, 2018 Palmeiras, 2019 Flamengo, 2021 Atlético-MG, 2022 Palmeiras, 2003 Cruzeiro. If any fails, investigate dedup.\n  - Libertadores finals: 2013 winner Atlético-MG (beat Olimpia). 2019 Flamengo (beat River). In data: 2013 final rows show Olimpia-PAR 2-0 Atlético-MG, Atlético-MG 2-0 Olimpia-PAR — decided on penalties (not in data). My \"find finals\" tool just lists final matches; fine.\n  - Copa do Brasil 2021? not in... Cup data 2012-2021. \n\n  Also the example in TASK.md: \"2012-05-27: Santos 8-0 Bolivar (Libertadores)\" — biggest wins example. Actual: Santos 8-0 Bolívar in 2012? Hmm, that was Copa Libertadores 2012... but Libertadores data starts 2013! So that example is illustrative only (\"provided data\" refers to their reference). My biggest_wins tool will compute from actual data; tests assert e.g. some 6-0 or bigger exists, and that margins compute correctly. Let me find actual biggest wins in data during dev.\n\n  Let me also double check: FIFA file — does it have Flamengo, Palmeiras, Corinthians, São Paulo, Vasco? The earlier grep printed only first 30 matches (unsorted Counter order). Let me get the definitive list of Brazilian clubs in FIFA data by cross-referencing with team entities from match data. I'll do this during implementation dev-testing.\n\n  **JSON output format**: Tools return structured JSON (dict) that gets json.dumps'd into text content. Spec's \"answer format\" examples are prose — but for MCP tools, structured JSON is standard and LLM-friendly. I'll return JSON objects with the key fields, plus a human-readable \"summary\" string inside the JSON? Hmm — tools/call content text. I'll return pretty JSON; include \"summary\" text fields where natural (like head-to-head summary line). Keep it clean.\n\n  Now the module layout:\n\n  ```\n  server.py                    # entry point: MCP stdio loop; --list-tools/--invoke for manual testing\n  brazilian_soccer/\n    __init__.py                # version, package exports\n    normalize.py               # team name + date + number normalization, aliases\n    models.py                  # Match, Player dataclasses (frozen? plain), helpers\n    repository.py              # DataRepository: loads 6 CSVs, builds entities, dedup, indexes\n    queries.py                 # query layer: search_matches, head_to_head, team_stats, search_players, standings, biggest_wins, stats_summary, find_team, derbies, competition_info, player_detail\n    mcp/\n      __init__.py\n      protocol.py              # JSON-RPC framing, dispatch loop (server class)\n      tools.py                 # tool schemas + dispatch wiring to queries\n  tests/\n    conftest.py\n    test_match_queries.py\n    test_team_queries.py\n    test_player_queries.py\n    test_competition_queries.py\n    test_statistics.py\n    test_normalization.py\n    test_mcp_protocol.py\n    test_performance.py\n  ```\n\n  Hmm, maybe simpler: `brazilian_soccer/server.py` containing protocol + tools, and root `server.py` shim that runs it. The context-block comment requirement from prompts.txt: \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — that was part of the sample prompt in prompts.txt, which says to ignore it. But TASK.md is my spec; it doesn't require context blocks. However, being a benchmark, mirroring the guide's spirit... TASK.md says nothing about context comments. I'll write good module docstrings (which serve that purpose) — detailed context at the start of every file. That satisfies both without violating \"no comments unless asked\"... well, the system prompt says don't add comments unless asked; module docstrings are standard Python practice though. I'll use module docstrings (they're documentation, idiomatic) and keep inline comments minimal.\n\n  Now the derby table for \"Show me all derbies in 2023\":\n  Known Brazilian derbies (normalized key pairs):\n  - Fla-Flu: flamengo vs fluminense\n  - Clássico dos Milhões: flamengo vs vasco\n  - Majestoso: corinthians vs sao paulo\n  - Choque-Rei: palmeiras vs sao paulo\n  - Derby Paulista: palmeiras vs corinthians\n  - Gre-Nal: gremio vs internacional\n  - Ba-Vi: bahia vs vitoria (ba)\n  - Clássico-Rei (Ceará): ceara vs fortaleza\n  - Atletiba: atletico pr vs coritiba\n  - Clássico da Amizade? skip\n  - Clássico Vovô: botafogo vs vasco? (actually Botafogo x Vasco is \"Clássico dos Gigantes\"? Hmm: Botafogo-Vasco = \"Clássico dos Gigantes\"? Let me keep well-known ones: Fla-Flu, Milhões, Majestoso, Choque-Rei, Derby Paulista, Gre-Nal, Ba-Vi, Atletiba, Clássico-Rei (CE), Come-Fogo? no. Also \"Clássico Alvinegro\"? skip.)\n  Include a name for each pair.\n\n  Now the entity resolution design (concrete):\n\n  ```python\n  STATE_ABBRS = {all 27 UF codes}\n\n  def _strip_accents(s): NFKD → ascii\n  def _base_clean(name):\n      # 1. unicode normalize, strip accents, lowercase\n      # 2. remove content in parentheses (incl. the parens)\n      # 3. replace '–'/'—'/'.' etc with space\n      # 4. apply full-name aliases (multi-word replaces):\n      ALIASES = {\n        \"sport club corinthians paulista\": \"corinthians sp\",\n        \"clube de regatas do flamengo\": \"flamengo rj\",\n        ...\n      }\n      # applied when the whole cleaned string equals key OR contains? Whole-string equality safest; plus substring for things like \"atletico mineiro\".\n      # 5. detect trailing \"- UF\" / \" UF\" state code → strip, remember\n      # 6. collapse separators to single spaces; strip legal tokens: {\"fc\",\"ec\",\"sc\",\"acb\",\"sa\",\"spfc\"?} — hmm careful\n  ```\n\n  Wait, legal token removal: \"Sao Paulo FC\"? not in data. FIFA has \"Grêmio\" (simple), \"Atlético Mineiro\", \"Atlético Paranaense\", \"Sport Club do Recife\", \"Ceará Sporting Club\", \"Bahia\" (or \"EC Bahia\"?). Match files have \"Bahia-BA\"/\"Bahia\". Let me check FIFA club values for Brazilians precisely. And BR-Football has \"America FC Natal\", \"American?\"; whatever — targeted aliases.\n\n  Let me define ALIASES mapping cleaned full string → canonical \"base state\" form:\n  - \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico mg\"\n  - \"atletico paranaense\" → \"atletico pr\" ; \"athletico paranaense\" → \"atletico pr\"\n  - \"atletico goianiense\" → \"atletico go\"\n  - \"sport club do recife\" → \"sport pe\"; \"sport recife\" → \"sport pe\"\n  - \"ceara sporting club\" → \"ceara\"\n  - \"clube de regatas do flamengo\" → \"flamengo\"\n  - \"sport club corinthians paulista\" → \"corinthians\"\n  - \"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\"\n  - \"sao paulo fc\" → \"sao paulo\"\n  - \"gremio fbpa\" → \"gremio\" (if needed)\n  - \"botafogo fr\" → \"botafogo\"\n  - \"vasco da gama\" → \"vasco\"\n  - \"america fc natal\" → \"america rn\"; \"america rn\" → \"america rn\" (auto)\n  - \"vila nova fc\"? \"vila nova go\"? → \"vila nova go\"\n  - \"ec bahia\" / \"esporte clube bahia\" → \"bahia\"\n  - \"ec vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\"\n  - \"chapecoense\"? fine.\n  - \"parana clube\"? \"parana\"? → \"parana\"\n  - \"criciuma\"? fine.\n  - \"internacional\"? \"sc internacional\" → \"internacional\"\n  - \"avai\"? fine. \"fortaleza esporte clube\"? if appears → \"fortaleza\" (spec mentions \"Fortaleza Esporte Clube\" as an example of cedilla/accent text! So include alias \"fortaleza esporte clube\" → \"fortaleza\").\n\n  Then the key = base + (\" \" + state if state else \"\"). Entity id formation happens after loading: collect map from stripped-suffix base → set of states seen. If len(states) > 1 → all entities get qualified ids (base + state); if the state is missing for a variant (e.g., \"Flamengo\" no suffix, but only RJ ever) → unqualified id \"flamengo\". If ambiguous base and no state → resolution returns all entities with that base.\n\n  Hmm wait, one more subtlety: \"atletico pr\" base \"atletico\" state \"pr\", and alias \"atletico paranaense\" → base \"atletico\" state \"pr\" (I'd write alias value as \"atletico pr\" which then gets parsed? I should apply aliases BEFORE state detection so alias targets like \"atletico mg\" get their state detected. Order: clean → alias (whole-string replace on cleaned string) → state-detect → key.\n\n  But \"Atlético-MG\" from Brasileirao cleans to \"atletico mg\" → state-detect: trailing \"mg\" → base \"atletico\", state \"mg\". \"Atlético Mineiro\" (FIFA) → alias → \"atletico mg\" → same. \n\n  \"America MG\" (BR-Football) → \"america mg\" → base \"america\" state \"mg\". \"America RN\" → base \"america\" state \"rn\". Ambiguous base \"america\" → qualified entities. User \"america-mg\" → resolves to \"america mg\". \n\n  \"Athletico-PR\" (Brasileirao 2019+) → \"athletico pr\" → base \"athletico\" state \"pr\" → alias base-level \"athletico\" → \"atletico\"?? I need base alias too: after state detection, base \"athletico\" → canonical base \"atletico\". Add BASE_ALIASES = {\"athletico\"