{"type":"text","timestamp":1788237491408,"sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","part":{"id":"prt_05b425aac001pnQF3v2Wr6kAeP","messageID":"msg_05b3a764f001xzOaSPbYHFCZWb","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","type":"text","text":"All data explored and understood. Now I'll build the implementation. Design summary:\n\n- **stdlib-only** MCP server (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio, per MCP spec) — the venv has no `mcp` package, so zero-dependency is safest\n- **Package layout**: `brazilian_soccer/` (normalize → models → repository → queries → tools → protocol) + `server.py` entry point\n- **Key data engineering**: cross-file team entity resolution (suffix/alias/accent normalization with disambiguation for `America-MG` vs `America-RN` etc.), multi-format date parsing, per-competition-season source selection with stat merging (3 files overlap for Serie A)\n- **BDD/GWT pytest suites** asserting known facts (e.g., 2019 champion Flamengo) to validate the wholepipeline","time":{"start":1788237470380,"end":1788237491406}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788237491413,"sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-aaeb602e4091f7b6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"Normalization helpers for heterogeneous Brazilian soccer datasets.\n\nContext: the six bundled Kaggle CSV files describe the same real-world\nclubs under many different spellings (\"Palmeiras-SP\", \"Palmeiras\",\n\"Sociedade Esportiva Palmeiras\", \"Grêmio\" vs \"Gremio-RS\", \"Athletico-PR\"\nvs \"Atlético-PR\"), record dates in ISO, ISO+time and Brazilian day-first\nformats, and store numbers as ints, floats or the sentinel \"NA\"/\"-\".\nEvery raw CSV value is funneled through the helpers below so that team\nmatching, grouping and sorting behave consistently across all files.\n\nPublic API:\n    strip_accents(value)          -> ASCII-folded string\n    clean_text(value)             -> canonical comparison form for names\n    split_team(name)              -> (base, qualifier) pair for a team name\n    parse_date(value)             -> (date, \"HH:MM\") or (None, None)\n    parse_int(value)              -> int or None\n    is_nothing(value)             -> True for empty/\"NA\"/\"-\"/\"None\" cells\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport re\nimport unicodedata\nfrom datetime import date, datetime\n\nBRAZILIAN_STATE_ABBRS = frozenset(\n    {\n        \"ac\", \"al\", \"ap\", \"am\", \"ba\", \"ce\", \"df\", \"es\", \"go\", \"ma\",\n        \"mt\", \"ms\", \"mg\", \"pa\", \"pb\", \"pr\", \"pe\", \"pi\", \"rj\", \"rn\",\n        \"rs\", \"ro\", \"rr\", \"sc\", \"sp\", \"se\", \"to\",\n    }\n)\n\nCOUNTRY_ABBRS = frozenset(\n    {\n        \"arg\", \"bol\", \"bra\", \"chi\", \"col\", \"ecu\", \"equ\", \"par\", \"per\",\n        \"uru\", \"ven\", \"mex\", \"usa\", \"crc\", \"gua\", \"hon\", \"slv\", \"pan\",\n        \"jam\", \"can\", \"tri\",\n    }\n)\n\nQUALIFIER_ABBRS = BRAZILIAN_STATE_ABBRS | COUNTRY_ABBRS\n\nPAREN_STATE_NAMES = {\n    \"minas gerais\": \"mg\",\n    \"rio de janeiro\": \"rj\",\n    \"sao paulo\": \"sp\",\n    \"rio grande do sul\": \"rs\",\n    \"santa catarina\": \"sc\",\n    \"parana\": \"pr\",\n    \"bahia\": \"ba\",\n    \"ceara\": \"ce\",\n    \"pernambuco\": \"pe\",\n    \"goias\": \"go\",\n    \"paraiba\": \"pb\",\n}\n\nFULL_NAME_ALIASES = {\n    \"sport club corinthians paulista\": \"corinthians sp\",\n    \"clube de regatas do flamengo\": \"flamengo rj\",\n    \"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\": \"palmeiras sp\",\n    \"sao paulo fc\": \"sao paulo sp\",\n    \"santos fc\": \"santos sp\",\n    \"fluminense fc\": \"fluminense rj\",\n    \"botafogo fr\": \"botafogo rj\",\n    \"sc internacional\": \"internacional rs\",\n    \"gremio fbpa\": \"gremio rs\",\n    \"atletico mineiro\": \"atletico mg\",\n    \"clube atletico mineiro\": \"atletico mg\",\n    \"atletico paranaense\": \"atletico pr\",\n    \"athletico paranaense\": \"atletico pr\",\n    \"atletico goianiense\": \"atletico go\",\n    \"sport club do recife\": \"sport pe\",\n    \"sport recife\": \"sport pe\",\n    \"ceara sporting club\": \"ceara ce\",\n    \"fortaleza esporte clube\": \"fortaleza ce\",\n    \"ec bahia\": \"bahia ba\",\n    \"esporte clube bahia\": \"bahia ba\",\n    \"ec vitoria\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"esporte clube vitoria\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"vitoria\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"vitoria fc\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"botafogo\": \"botafogo rj\",\n    \"guarani\": \"guarani sp\",\n    \"america fc\": \"america mg\",\n    \"america fc natal\": \"america rn\",\n    \"america rj\": \"america rj\",\n}\n\nBASE_ALIASES = {\n    \"athletico\": \"atletico\",\n    \"vasco da gama\": \"vasco\",\n    \"america fc\": \"america\",\n}\n\n_PAREN_RE = re.compile(r\"\\(([^)]*)\\)\")\n_PUNCT_RE = re.compile(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\")\n_SPACE_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s+\")\n\n_DATETIME_FORMATS = (\n    \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\",\n    \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\",\n    \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\",\n    \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M:%S\",\n)\n_DATE_FORMATS = (\"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\")\n\n_NOTHING = frozenset({\"\", \"na\", \"n/a\", \"-\", \"none\", \"null\", \"?\"})\n\n\ndef is_nothing(value) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"True for blank/missing CSV cells of any common flavour.\"\"\"\n    if value is None:\n        return True\n    return str(value).strip().lower() in _NOTHING\n\n\ndef strip_accents(value: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Fold accented characters down to their ASCII equivalents.\"\"\"\n    decomposed = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", value)\n    return \"\".join(ch for ch in decomposed if not unicodedata.combining(ch))\n\n\ndef clean_text(value) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Canonical comparison form for names: ASCII, lowercase, no punctuation.\n\n    Parenthesised content is dropped unless it carries a state/country\n    qualifier (e.g. \"Nacional (URU)\" keeps the \"uru\" token, while\n    \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\" loses it).\n    \"\"\"\n    if value is None:\n        return \"\"\n    text = strip_accents(str(value)).strip().lower()\n    if not text:\n        return \"\"\n\n    def _paren(match: re.Match) -> str:\n        inner = _PUNCT_RE.sub(\" \", match.group(1)).strip()\n        if inner in PAREN_STATE_NAMES:\n            return \" \" + PAREN_STATE_NAMES[inner]\n        if inner in QUALIFIER_ABBRS:\n            return \" \" + inner\n        return \" \"\n\n    text = _PAREN_RE.sub(_paren, text)\n    text = _PUNCT_RE.sub(\" \", text)\n    return _SPACE_RE.sub(\" \", text).strip()\n\n\ndef _split_qualifier(text: str) -> tuple[str, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"Split a trailing state/country code off a cleaned name.\"\"\"\n    tokens = text.split(\" \")\n    if len(tokens) > 1 and tokens[-1] in QUALIFIER_ABBRS:\n        return \" \".join(tokens[:-1]), tokens[-1]\n    return text, None\n\n\ndef split_team(name) -> tuple[str, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"Reduce a raw team/club spelling to a (base, qualifier) pair.\n\n    \"Palmeiras-SP\" -> (\"palmeiras\", \"sp\"); \"Palmeiras\" -> (\"palmeiras\", None);\n    \"Atlético Paranaense\" -> (\"atletico\", \"pr\"); \"Botafogo-PB\" ->\n    (\"botafogo\", \"pb\"). The pair is stable across all six datasets so that\n    equal pairs always denote the same real-world club.\n    \"\"\"\n    cleaned = clean_text(name)\n    if not cleaned:\n        return \"\", None\n    cleaned = FULL_NAME_ALIASES.get(cleaned, cleaned)\n    base, qualifier = _split_qualifier(cleaned)\n    base = BASE_ALIASES.get(base, base)\n    if base != cleaned and base in FULL_NAME_ALIASES:\n        base = FULL_NAME_ALIASES[base]\n        base, extra = _split_qualifier(base)\n        base = BASE_ALIASES.get(base, base)\n        qualifier = qualifier or extra\n    return base, qualifier\n\n\ndef parse_date(value) -> tuple[date | None, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"Parse the date formats used across the datasets.\n\n    Accepts \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"2023-09-24\" and \"29/03/2003\".\n    Returns (date, \"HH:MM\") with the time part missing when unknown.\n    \"\"\"\n    if is_nothing(value):\n        return None, None\n    text = str(value).strip()\n    for fmt in _DATETIME_FORMATS:\n        try:\n            stamp = datetime.strptime(text, fmt)\n            return stamp.date(), stamp.strftime(\"%H:%M\")\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n    for fmt in _DATE_FORMATS:\n        try:\n            stamp = datetime.strptime(text, fmt)\n            return stamp.date(), None\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n    return None, None\n\n\ndef parse_int(value) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"Parse goals/round numbers stored as int, float or numeric text.\"\"\"\n    if is_nothing(value):\n        return None\n    try:\n        return int(float(str(value).strip()))\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        return None\n","filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d725b25539e0/brazilian_soccer/normalize.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-x26yewvv/retort-d725b25539e0/brazilian_soccer/normalize.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer/normalize.py","time":{"start":1788237491403,"end":1788237491410}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have complete clarity. Interesting notes:\n\n1. **Libertadores**: both \"-PAR\" suffixes and \"(PAR)\" parens for the same clubs (\"Nacional (PAR)\" AND \"Nacional-URU\" AND \"Nacional (URU)\" — same club different formats!). So paren-strip + country-code strip unifies.\n\n2. **Brasileirao teams**: unaccented (\"Avai-SC\", \"Ceara-CE\", \"Goias-GO\", \"Gremio-RS\", \"Vitoria-BA\", \"Sao Paulo-SP\", \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\"). Note \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" — state strip → \"vasco da gama\" → need alias → \"vasco\". And novo has \"Vasco\". So alias \"vasco da gama\" → \"vasco\" (or keep \"vasco da gama\" as canonical and alias \"vasco\" → it? For cross-join, either way consistent alias needed. I'll canonicalize to \"vasco\".)\n\n3. **BR-Football**: 'EC Vitoria', 'Vitoria', 'Vitoria EC' (all BA presumably), 'Vitoria ES' (ES club), 'Vitoria da Conquista' (different club!). So aliases: \"ec vitoria\"→\"vitoria ba\", \"vitoria ec\"→\"vitoria ba\", \"vitoria\"→? ambiguous base (ba, es, and 'vitoria da conquista' is a distinct base so fine). Bare \"vitoria\" in BR-Football is likely BA (Serie A/B). Hmm — \"Vitoria\" bare: which state? In Serie B 2014-2019, Vitória-BA played. Vitória-ES played Serie B 2018? Hmm. If I alias bare \"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\", matches go to BA club. Is that right? Vitória-ES (Rio Branco-Vitória?) — actually the ES club is \"Rio Branco\"? No — \"Vitória-ES\" does exist (Vitória Futebol Clube de Vitória? no...). Hmm, let me check which seasons/tournaments \"Vitoria ES\" appears in, to decide. If \"Vitoria ES\" appears only in Serie C, and bare \"Vitoria\" appears in Serie A/B, they're likely both BA in Serie A/B... wait no — if Vitória-ES is a real distinct club appearing as \"Vitoria ES\", then bare \"Vitoria\" alongside \"EC Vitoria\"/\"Vitoria EC\" in the same file is probably always the BA one. Let me check: BR-Football Serie A has Vitoria? Vitória-BA was in Serie A 2017, 2018; Serie B 2019, 2020?; Vitória-ES never Serie A. So bare \"Vitoria\" in Serie A = BA. I'll alias bare \"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\" and \"ec vitoria\"/\"vitoria ec\" → \"vitoria ba\". And \"vitoria es\" stays qualified (base vitoria, state es).\n\n   Similarly America: \"America FC Natal\" → \"america rn\", \"America MG\" → \"america mg\", \"America RN\" → \"america rn\", \"Americano RJ\" → \"americano rj\" (distinct club, base \"americano\" — unique, fine).\n   \n   \"Sport Recife\" → \"sport pe\" (alias), \"Sport\" (novo) → base \"sport\", state pe? novo \"Sport\" has no suffix. Brasileirao \"Sport-PE\" → base \"sport\" state \"pe\". BR-Football \"Sport Recife\" → alias → \"sport recife\"→? I'll alias \"sport recife\" → \"sport pe\". And bare \"sport\" (novo) → base \"sport\" no state; entities: base \"sport\" states={pe} (from Sport-PE and Sport Recife) + bare. Unique state pe → entity \"sport pe\"? Hmm wait — my entity-id rule: if base maps to multiple distinct states → qualified; if single state (or none) → unqualified base id. For \"sport\": states seen = {pe, None} → single non-None state → id \"sport\". Then FIFA \"Sport Club do Recife\" → alias → \"sport pe\" → base \"sport\" state \"pe\" → same entity. But bare \"sport\" from novo → base \"sport\" state None → entity \"sport\" too. All join to entity \"sport\". \n\n   Wait, but that breaks for \"america\": states={mg, rn} → entities \"america mg\", \"america rn\". A bare \"America\" (if it appeared without suffix anywhere) would be ambiguous — resolution would pick... I'll resolve bare ambiguous bases by: if exactly one entity has matches in the requested competition context, pick it; else return disambiguation. For the FIFA club \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" — paren \"(Minas Gerais)\" → contains \"Minas Gerais\" = MG. I could map parenthetical state names to codes: \"(Minas Gerais)\" → mg! Nice: parse state names {minas gerais: mg, rio de janeiro: rj, sao paulo: sp, ...}. Simpler: alias exact \"america fc\" → \"america mg\" (FIFA-specific). But \"America FC Natal\" → \"america fc natal\" ≠ \"america fc\". OK exact alias \"america fc\" → \"america mg\" works.\n\n4. **BR-Football Serie A 2021 has 491 matches!** That's MORE than 380 — it includes duplicated matches? 491 > 380. Weird. Maybe it includes some Copa? Or 2021 Serie A had 380 matches but data has extra? Hmm, maybe it double-counts or includes playoff? Serie A 2021: 20 teams double round robin = 380. 491 suggests data quality issues (duplicates within the file?). And 2020: 264 (incomplete — COVID year, data collection stopped?), 2014: 281 (partial). 2015: 378, 2016: 379, 2022: 379 — near-complete. \n\n   So BR-Football is partially complete and sometimes over-complete. My dedup (competition, season, date, teams, score) also handles intra-file duplicates if any. For standings, partial data yields wrong tables — so standings MUST prefer complete sources. Preference order for \"Brasileirão Serie A\": Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022, 380 each — complete!) > novo (2003-2019) > BR-Football (2014-2023, partial/over). For 2023: only BR-Football → standings 2023 computed from 377 matches — close to complete; note \"partial\" in response if count != 380? I can include a data-completeness note: if a season has < 90% of expected matches... expected varies (2003: 552 matches 24 teams? 2003 had 46 games/team...). Simpler: standings response includes \"matches_counted\" and a \"note\" if odd. I'll add note when matches < 300 for Serie A? Meh — I'll add note when team count != 20 or matches != expected for that season shape. Keep it simple: include matches count, let consumer judge. Actually for safety, add a \"data_complete\" heuristic: every team plays same number of matches. If not, note \"partial data\".\n\n   2021's 491: after dedup, if extra matches are true duplicates (same date/teams/score), they collapse. If they're distinct rows (e.g., different dates?), they'd remain and skew standings for 2021 — but 2021 Serie A also exists in Brasileirao_Matches (380, complete), so preference keeps the good one and the BR-Football dups merge/drop. Wait — my dedup: I iterate sources in preference order; a later-source row whose key exists is dropped (stats merged). BR-Football rows with keys NOT matching (e.g., wrong dates) would be ADDED as extra matches → polluting 2021 standings with extra matches! Danger!\n\n   Hmm. How to handle: for seasons where a preferred source already provides a \"complete\" season (matches == teams*(teams-1) i.e. full double round-robin, or matches >= 380 for 20 teams), skip lower-preference sources entirely for that (competition, season). That's robust: complete season data from a better source → ignore the noisy source. For partial preferred coverage, let lower sources fill in (e.g., 2014 Brasileirao has 380 complete → BR-Football 2014 (281) fully skipped. 2023: no preferred source → BR-Football used, 377 matches).\n\n   Completeness check: n_teams*2*(n_teams-1) matches for double round robin; 2003 (24 teams, 552) and 2005 (462 = 22 teams? no wait 2005 had 22 teams 462 matches. Yes!) fit the formula. So: complete if matches == n_teams * (n_teams - 1) * 2... hmm 2003: 24*23*2 = 1104 ≠ 552. 2003 Brasileirão: 24 teams, single round robin + playoffs? 2003 was 46 rounds 24 teams = 552 = 24*23. Single round robin! 2005: 22 teams 42 rounds = 462 = 22*21 single. 2006+: 20 teams 38 rounds = 380 = 20*19 single round robin... wait double round robin of 20 teams is 38 rounds 380 matches; 20*19 = 380. Both formulas coincide? No: double round robin 20 teams = 2*(20-1)*... each team plays 19 others twice = 38 games; total matches = 20*38/2 = 380 = 20*19*2/2? 20*19=380 yes coincidence: n(n-1) = 380 for n=20 single-round... single round robin n=20 → 190 matches. 380 = 2×190. And n(n-1) for 24 = 552. So 2003 (24 teams, 552) = single round robin? No wait 24 teams single round robin = 24×23/2 = 276. 552 = 276×2 = double round robin = n(n-1). OK so formula: double round robin matches = n(n-1). 24 teams → 552 ✓. 22 teams → 462 ✓. 20 → 380 ✓. All double round robin. \n\n   So completeness = (matches == n_teams * (n_teams - 1)) for Serie A. If complete from a preferred source → skip lower sources for that season. If not complete, still prefer higher source rows and add non-duplicate lower rows? That could add garbage (BR-Football 2021 had 491 — extra beyond real). Ugh. Alternative simpler robust rule: for each (competition, season), use EXACTLY ONE source — the highest-preference source that has data for it. No mixing within a season. Mixing only risks pollution; single-source is predictable:\n   - Serie A 2003-2019: novo? No wait preference: Brasileirao_Matches covers 2012-2022 (380 each, complete). novo covers 2003-2019. For 2012-2019 both have complete data — pick Brasileirao_Matches (preferred: round numbers + datetime). For 2003-2011 only novo. 2023 only BR-Football.\n   - Hmm, but then novo's Arena/stadium data unused for 2012-2019, and its 2003-2011 matches keep stadiums. Fine.\n   - Copa do Brasil 2012-2021: Brazilian_Cup file (rounds!). 2022-2023: BR-Football.\n   - Serie B/C: BR-Football only (2014-2023).\n   - Libertadores: its file.\n   \n   This per-(competition, season) single-source policy is clean, predictable, and I'll expose `source` on each match and document it. For extended stats (corners/shots) from BR-Football: they'd be lost for seasons where another source is preferred... For matches where BR-Football has the SAME match (same date/teams/score) I can attach stats to the kept match (merge without keeping the dup). That keeps extended stats available wherever BR-Football covers the same match, without polluting match lists. \n\n   Wait, but actually — should search_matches be able to include ALL raw rows (e.g., user asks \"show everything in BR-Football-Dataset.csv\")? The success criteria says \"All 6 CSV files are loadable and queryable\". With per-season single-source, BR-Football rows for 2014-2022 Serie A are \"shadowed\". They're still queryable via... hmm. I'll add a `source` filter param to search_matches (e.g., source=\"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\") that searches RAW rows (all of them), while default search uses the curated/deduped set. That satisfies \"all files loadable and queryable\" fully. Good compromise.\n\n5. Also novo has 'Vasco' bare and Brasileirao has 'Vasco da Gama-RJ' → alias \"vasco da gama\" → \"vasco\".\n\n6. Note the Cup 2012 has 6 rounds, and 2021 has 4. Finals = max round per season. OK.\n\n7. Fifa club \"Paraná\" → base \"parana\" no state. Match data \"Parana-PR\" (Brasileirao) / \"Paraná\" (novo) → base \"parana\" state pr → single state pr → entity \"parana\". Joins fine.\n\n8. FIFA \"Chapecoense\" joins \"Chapecoense-SC\". FIFA \"Bahia\" joins \"Bahia-BA\". FIFA \"Grêmio\" joins \"Gremio-RS\"/\"Grêmio\". FIFA \"Santos\" joins \"Santos-SP\"/\"Santos\". FIFA \"Botafogo\" joins \"Botafogo-RJ\". FIFA \"Internacional\" joins \"Internacional-RS\". FIFA \"Cruzeiro\" joins \"Cruzeiro-MG\". FIFA \"Fluminense\" joins \"Fluminense-RJ\". FIFA \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → alias → \"ceara\" joins \"Ceara-CE\"/\"Ceará\". FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" → alias → \"atletico mg\". FIFA \"Atlético Paranaense\" → alias → \"atletico pr\". FIFA \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → paren-strip → \"america fc\" → alias → \"america mg\". FIFA \"Sport Club do Recife\" → alias → \"sport pe\"→ entity sport. FIFA \"Vitória\" → alias → \"vitoria ba\". FIFA \"Paraná\" → \"parana\".\n\nNow entity id rule refinement: id = base if base has ≤1 distinct state across all loaded data; else base+\" \"+state per state. \"vitoria\": states {ba, es} → \"vitoria ba\", \"vitoria es\". Bare \"Vitoria\" alias → \"vitoria ba\" explicit. novo \"Vitória\" (no state)! → base \"vitoria\" state None → contributes state None. States = {ba, es, None} → ambiguous → qualified entities \"vitoria ba\", \"vitoria es\"; where does bare \"Vitória\" from novo go?? It has no state — I must map it via alias: exact-string alias \"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\" (same as FIFA). Add to ALIASES: \"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\". Since in Brazilian match data, bare Vitória = Vitória-BA (Série A club). ✓.\n\nSimilarly bare \"America\"? Does bare \"America\" appear? BR-Football has America MG/RN/Natal only. novo has América-MG/América-RN. Cup \"América - MG\". No bare America. OK.\n\n\"Guaraní (PAR)\" and \"Guaraní-PAR\" → both \"guarani\". ✓ (base unique — the Paraguayan Guaraní; no Brazilian Guaraní? novo has \"Guarani\" (Campinas)!! CONFLICT: novo \"Guarani\" (SP, Campinas) vs Libertadores \"Guaraní-PAR\" (Paraguay). Base \"guarani\" states {None(novo, actually let me check novo \"Guarani\" — it's Guarani-SP? The novo list shows 'Guarani' bare (2003-2019 Serie A/B — Guarani Campinas played 2003?). Brasileirao_Matches? Not in list (2003 only novo). So base \"guarani\" states {pr, None} → ambiguous → \"guarani pr\" and bare... my rule: qualified per state; bare None state → hmm, the None-state variant forms its own... \n\n    Refine rule: states set S (excluding None). If |S| <= 1 → single entity id=base (bare variants join). If |S| > 1 → entity per state; a bare variant with no state → goes to... I need a tiebreak. Options: (a) bare joins the entity with most matches for that base; (b) bare becomes its own entity \"guarani\" (splitting from \"guarani pr\" — but then Brasileirao \"Guarani-SP\"?? if it appeared it'd be \"guarani sp\" a third). For \"guarani\": novo bare (Campinas, SP) vs Libertadores \"Guarani-PAR\". If bare → own entity \"guarani\" (Campinas) and \"guarani pr\" separate — CORRECT! They're different clubs. And within Libertadores all Guaraní have -PAR → consistent. Within novo bare → entity \"guarani\" ✓.\n    \n    But then for \"vitoria\" bare (novo) → alias handled explicitly to \"vitoria ba\" so no issue. And \"sport\" bare → alias? states {pe} → single → id \"sport\" ✓ no problem.\n    \n    So rule: if |S|>1, bare variants get entity base itself. \"atletico\": states {mg, go, pr} — bare \"Atlético\" appears anywhere? Libertadores \"Atlético-MG\"/\"Atlético-PR\" have suffixes. BR-Football \"Atletico Alagoinhas\" (BA, distinct base \"atletico alagoinhas\" — wait, that's a club whose name STARTS with Atletico but full base differs — fine, different base string). Bare base \"atletico\" exactly — probably doesn't appear. OK.\n\n    Hmm wait, one more: \"nacional\": Libertadores \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Nacional-URU\", \"Nacional (PAR)\", \"Nacional-PAR\"? states after country-strip: uru, par → |S|>1 → entities \"nacional uru\", \"nacional par\". Fine, distinct clubs ✓.\n\n    And \"barcelona\": \"Barcelona-EQU\" → \"barcelona equ\" → country strip → \"barcelona\". Unique ✓. \"colo-colo\" → base \"colo colo\"? My separator collapse turns hyphens into spaces: \"colo-colo\" → \"colo colo\". Hmm — is that desired? \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" → \"vasco da gama rj\" → state strip → \"vasco da gama\" → alias → \"vasco\". \"Colo-Colo\" → \"colo colo\" stays. Consistent since same club always spelled \"Colo-Colo\". Fine.\n\n    \"River Plate-URU\" → \"river plate\" (Uruguayan River Plate, distinct from Argentine \"River Plate\"? In Libertadores data probably only the URU one appears with suffix... or Argentine River appears as \"River Plate\" bare? If both appear: bare → ambiguous... let me not chase every case; the disambiguation machinery handles it: bare + multiple states → bare gets own entity; user queries resolve via variants.\n\nAlso Brasileirao \"Csa-AL\" vs novo \"CSA\" — base \"csa\" state {al, None} → single non-None → id \"csa\" ✓.\n\n\"Cuiaba-MT\" ✓. \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" → base \"red bull bragantino\" ✓.\n\n**Now the entity → display name**: collect raw variants per entity, pick most frequent (ties → longest?). E.g. entity \"flamengo\": raws {\"Flamengo-RJ\"(×N), \"Flamengo\"(×M), ...} → display \"Flamengo-RJ\" if most frequent. For answer text, better display might be \"Flamengo\". Whatever — display = most common raw, good enough; tests can assert entity resolution not display.\n\n**Competition canonicalization** (user-facing names + aliases):\n- \"Brasileirão Serie A\" (aliases: brasileirao, serie a, campeonato brasileiro, brasieleirao serie a)\n- \"Brasileirão Serie B\" (serie b)\n- \"Brasileirão Serie C\" (serie c)\n- \"Copa do Brasil\" (copa do brasil, brazilian cup, copa)\n- \"Copa Libertadores\" (libertadores, libertadores cup, conmebol)\n\nNow, season type: int. Some Liberty 'NA' rows skipped (no goals). Skip matches with unparseable date AND unparseable goals? The NA Libertadores final row: skip (can't score it). I'll skip rows missing goals or date, count them in a data-quality report: repository.load_report with per-file {rows, loaded, skipped}.\n\n**Now statistics functions** — straightforward from deduped matches + source-selected matches.\n\nFor standings: per (competition, season) pick source by preference; compute table. Mark champion (row 1), relegation (last 4 when 20 teams Serie A).\n\n**Team stats**: filter deduped matches by entity (either side) + optional season/competition; compute overall + home/away splits + per-competition breakdown.\n\n**head_to_head**: entity pair; count wins for each + draws; goal totals; latest match first.\n\n**search_matches**: filters incl. team (either), home_team/away_team/opponent, competition, season, date range, venue (\"home\"/\"away\" for team), stage (Libertadores), round (Cup), limit, sort order. Returns matches with all fields.\n\n**biggest_wins**: margin sort desc, top N, optional competition/season.\n\n**stats_summary**: competition/season aggregates.\n\n**search_players**: name substring (normalized), nationality exact (normalized), club substring or entity-resolved (accept team entity names like \"atletico-mg\" → matches FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" via normalized club key alias — I'll normalize FIFA Club with the SAME team normalizer! Then club filter = resolved entity key OR raw substring). position exact code or group (GK/DEF/MID/FWD aliases goalkeeper etc.), min_overall, max_age, sort by overall desc default, limit.\n\n**player_detail**: name (normalized substring → best overall match) or ID. Return all fields incl. skills.\n\n**find_team(query)**: substring match over entity display names + variants + keys; return entities with match counts, states, variants.\n\n**derby_matches(season?, competition?)**: DERBIES list of (key_a, key_b, name). Filter matches where {home,away} == pair.\n\n**competition_info(competition?)**: per competition: seasons, matches, teams count, sources; if specific competition+season maybe standings summary? Keep: competition → seasons list + counts. Also `list_competitions` when no arg.\n\nTool list (MCP):\n1. search_matches\n2. head_to_head\n3. team_stats\n4. find_team\n5. search_players\n6. player_detail\n7. standings\n8. competition_info\n9. biggest_wins\n10. stats_summary\n11. derby_matches\n\nThat's a solid, spec-covering set. All required example questions answerable:\n- \"Show me all Flamengo vs Fluminense matches\" → search_matches or head_to_head ✓\n- \"What matches did Palmeiras play in 2023?\" → search_matches(team, season) ✓ (2023 Serie A partial from BR-Football + Copa 2023 + no Libertadores 2023)\n- \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" → search_matches(competition=\"Copa do Brasil\", stage/round=final)? Hmm — I need a \"finals\" concept. Add `stage` filter support: for Cup, \"final\" maps to max-round per season; for Libertadores \"final\" is a stage value. I'll add param `stage` free-text that matches Libertadores stage, plus special value \"final\" that uses per-season max round for Cup. ✓\n- \"When did Flamengo last play Corinthians?\" → head_to_head returns latest first ✓\n- \"Who is Gabriel Barbosa?\" → player_detail ✓ (check he's in FIFA data — \"Gabriel Barbosa\"? sample had 'Gabriel Barbosa'? I saw names: Neymar Jr, Casemiro, Coutinho, Marcelo, Thiago Silva, Ederson, Roberto Firmino, Alex Sandro, Douglas Costa, Fernandinho, Alisson, Paulinho... Gabriel Barbosa (\"Gabigol\") should be in Inter Milan/Santos in FIFA 19. I'll test.)\n- \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" → search_players(club=\"Flamengo\") → empty + helpful note listing covered Brazilian clubs. Hmm — I want to double check FIFA truly lacks Flamengo. Confirmed not in club list.\n- \"What is Corinthians' home record in 2022?\" → team_stats(team, season=2022, venue=\"home\") ✓\n- \"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023?\" → standings(serie a, 2023) sorted by GF? standings returns full table incl GF ✓\n- \"Compare Palmeiras and Santos head-to-head\" → head_to_head ✓\n- \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → standings ✓\n- \"Show the 2018 Copa Libertadores bracket\" → search_matches(competition, season, stage order)... \"bracket\" — knockout stages: search with stage filter or return grouped by stage. search_matches sorted by stage? I'll make search_matches for Libertadores return stage field; user can filter stage=\"quarterfinals\" etc. Also add optional group_by? Nah. ✓ good enough.\n- \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" → standings marks relegation zone ✓\n- \"What's the average goals per match in the Brasileirão?\" → stats_summary ✓\n- \"Which team has the best away record?\" → team_stats? No — needs cross-team ranking! Hmm: \"best away record\" across all teams. Add `best_teams` tool? Or team_rankings(metric=\"away_wins\"|\"away_points\"|\"home_points\"...)? Let me add a `team_rankings` tool: metric in {points, wins, draws, losses, goals_for, goals_against, win_rate, away_points, home_points, away_wins, home_win_rate,...}, optional competition, season. That covers \"which team has the best away record\" and \"which team scored most goals\". Wait — standings already covers per-season league tables. team_rankings generalizes across all matches. Add it — replaces nothing, adds analytical capability. 12 tools.\n- \"Show me the biggest wins\" → biggest_wins ✓\n- \"Show me all derbies in 2023\" → derby_matches ✓\n- \"What competitions has Palmeiras played in?\" → team_stats breakdown by competition ✓\n- \"Find all Brazilian players\" → search_players(nationality=\"Brazil\") ✓\n- \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" → search_players(club, sort overall) ✓ (empty for Flamengo but works for Grêmio etc.)\n- \"Show me all forwards from São Paulo FC\" → search_players(club=\"Sao Paulo\", position group forward) — empty (no São Paulo in FIFA 19) but works for e.g. Santos. ✓ behaviorally correct.\n- \"What was the score?\" (follow-up) → covered by search/head-to-head.\n- \"Compare the 2018 and 2019 seasons\" → stats_summary(season=2018) vs (2019) — two calls, or standings. ✓\n\nGreat — 12 tools: search_matches, head_to_head, team_stats, team_rankings, find_team, search_players, player_detail, standings, competition_info, biggest_wins, stats_summary, derby_matches.\n\n**Performance**: all in-memory. search over ~10-15k deduped matches (after source selection) or 24k raw. Fast.\n\nData sizes: raw rows: 4180+6886+1337+1255+10296+18207 = 42161 rows total. Load time ~0.5-1s. Fine.\n\n**Now write the code.** Files:\n\n1. `brazilian_soccer/__init__.py` — exports, version.\n2. `brazilian_soccer/normalize.py` — normalization utilities.\n3. `brazilian_soccer/models.py` — Match, Player dataclasses.\n4. `brazilian_soccer/repository.py` — DataRepository (load, entities, dedup, source selection, indexes).\n5. `brazilian_soccer/queries.py` — query layer.\n6. `brazilian_soccer/tools.py` — MCP tool schemas + dispatch (name → schema + handler). (Skip nested mcp/ package; single tools.py.)\n7. `brazilian_soccer/protocol.py` — JSON-RPC/MCP server loop.\n8. `server.py` — entry point.\n9. tests/...\n10. README.md update.\n\nLet me think carefully about the repository class structure:\n\n```python\nclass DataRepository:\n    def __init__(self, data_dir):\n        self.data_dir\n        self.matches: list[Match]        # curated (source-selected per comp/season, deduped)\n        self.raw_matches: list[Match]    # every loaded row (for source= queries)\n        self.players: list[Player]\n        self.load_report: dict\n        self.entities: dict[str, TeamEntity]  # id -> TeamEntity(display, variants: Counter, states)\n        self.competitions: dict[str, CompetitionInfo]  # canonical -> {seasons, sources, match_counts}\n\n    def resolve_team(self, query: str) -> list[TeamEntity]  # user-facing resolution\n    def matches_for_team(...)  etc. — maybe keep in queries.py\n```\n\nLoading procedure:\n1. Parse each file into Match objects (raw_matches), with entity resolution done after all teams known (two-phase: first pass collects raw names + builds entity registry, second pass assigns entity ids). Actually simpler: single pass building matches with `home_key`/`away_key` computed via team_key() (base+state string), THEN after all files loaded, compute ambiguity: base → set(states incl None). Then define entity_id(key_string) function; assign to matches.\n\nLet me define concretely:\n- `team_key(raw)` returns `(base, state_or_None)` — normalization + aliases + suffix detection.\n- After loading all raw names: `base_states: dict[base, set[str|None]]`.\n- `entity_id(base, state)`: s = base_states[base]; non_none = s - {None}; if len(non_none) <= 1 → base (collapse: any state variant maps to base?? wait NO — careful: if base \"flamengo\" states {rj, None}: entity \"flamengo\". If base \"sport\" states {pe, None}: \"sport\". If base \"america\" states {mg, rn}: TWO entities \"america mg\"/\"america rn\"; a hypothetical bare variant (None) maps to... base \"america\" itself as third entity? My rule: if len(non_none) > 1: bare → entity base? For \"guarani\" {pr, None}: non_none={pr} len 1 → entity \"guarani\" — WAIT that merges novo Guarani (Campinas) with Libertadores Guaraní-PAR into one entity! BAD. \n\nHmm. Rethink: \"guarani\" states {pr, None}: |non_none|=1 → my rule collapses all to \"guarani\" — merging the Paraguayan club with Campinas club. Both would count into same head-to-heads... Do they ever play each other? No (different competitions entirely). Stats mixing Guarani Campinas' Brasileirão matches with Guaraní-PAR's Libertadores matches under \"team_stats(guarani)\" — wrong but harmless-ish? It's incorrect data semantics though.\n\nBetter rule: entity id = base if ALL variants agree... Use: if s (all states incl None) == {None} → id=base (no state ever seen). If s == {x} (single non-None only, no None) → id=base+\" \"+x? Hmm but \"flamengo\" has {rj, None} — the None variants (Libertadores/novo/BR-Football bare \"Flamengo\") are the SAME club as Flamengo-RJ. Collapsing is CORRECT there. For \"guarani\" {pr, None}: None = Campinas (different club), pr = Paraguay. Collapsing wrong.\n\nCan't distinguish \"bare name = same club as the suffixed one\" vs \"bare name = different club\" without an alias table. Practical heuristic used by real projects on this data: within Brazilian competitions, the suffix-less name equals the club; ambiguity across states handled by state. Cross-country (Libertadores) bare/suffixed... \n\nPragmatic approach: the ONLY genuinely colliding bare cases in this data: guarani (Campinas vs PAR), nacional (URU vs PAR — both always suffixed/parens, never bare!), river plate? (only URU suffixed?), colo-colo (no state)... Let me enumerate actual collisions where a base has BOTH None and multiple/one states, and check which are same-club vs different-club. Small enough to fix with explicit aliases:\n\n- flamengo {rj, None} → same club → entity \"flamengo\" ✓ collapse.\n- guarani {pr, None} → DIFFERENT clubs → alias: novo \"Guarani\" → treat as \"guarani sp\"?? Hmm — is novo's Guarani Guarani-Campinas-SP? Yes (Guarani Futebol Clube, Campinas-SP). I'll add explicit alias \"guarani\" → \"guarani sp\"?? But then Brasileirao_Matches doesn't have Guarani at all, novo's becomes \"guarani sp\" unique state sp... wait base_states for base \"guarani\" would then be {sp, pr} → |non_none|=2 → qualified entities \"guarani sp\" (Campinas) & \"guarani pr\" (Paraguay). CORRECT! But the alias \"guarani\" → \"guarani sp\" would ALSO catch a hypothetical bare Paraguayan \"Guarani\" — doesn't exist bare in data. OK.\n\nHold on — but such an alias list is getting bespoke. The general rule + targeted exceptions is fine: general collapse rule (|non_none| ≤ 1 → base) + explicit aliases for known distinct clubs (guarani). Let me enumerate all bases with None + ≥1 state after implementing, and eyeball each. I'll do that during dev.\n\n- \"atletico\" {mg, go, pr} (never bare) → 3 entities ✓ correct clubs.\n- \"america\" {mg, rn} (never bare? Cup has \"América - MG\"... BR-Football \"America MG\"/\"America RN\"; novo América-MG/RN) → 2 entities ✓.\n- \"vitoria\" {ba, es, None?} — novo bare \"Vitória\" → alias to \"vitoria ba\" first. Then {ba, es} → 2 entities ✓.\n- \"sport\" {pe, None} → collapse ✓.\n- \"nautico\" {pe, None?} — \"Nautico-PE\" (Brasileirao) & novo \"Náutico\" bare → collapse \"nautico\" ✓ same club.\n- \"bahia\" {ba, None} ✓. \"ceara\" {ce, None} ✓. \"santos\" {sp, None} ✓. \"gremio\" {rs, None} ✓ (novo \"Grêmio\" bare, Brasileirao \"Gremio-RS\", FIFA \"Grêmio\" no state ✓). \"coritiba\" {pr, None} ✓. \"criciuma\" {sc, None} ✓. \"figueirense\" {sc, None} ✓. \"juventude\" {rs, None} ✓. \"goias\" {go, None} ✓. \"parana\" {pr, None} ✓. \"ponte preta\" {sp, None} ✓. \"portuguesa\" {sp, None} ✓. \"santa cruz\" {pe, None} ✓. \"sao paulo\" {sp, None} ✓ (BR-Football \"Sao Paulo\" bare ✓). \"corinthians\" {sp, None} ✓. \"palmeiras\" {sp, None} ✓. \"fluminense\" {rj, None} ✓. \"botafogo\" {rj, None, \"Botafogo-PB\"?} — BR-Football has \"Botafogo PB\"! (Paraíba club) → {rj, pb, None} → entities \"botafogo rj\", \"botafogo pb\". FIFA \"Botafogo\" bare → hmm! FIFA bare \"Botafogo\" = RJ club. But rule: |non_none|=2 → qualified; bare variant None → entity \"botafogo\"?? That would strand FIFA's Botafogo as separate entity from \"botafogo rj\". Add alias \"botafogo\" → \"botafogo rj\" (FIFA + novo bare Botafogo = RJ ✓ — novo has \"Botafogo-RJ\" per list. BR-Football bare \"Botafogo\"? It had \"Botafogo PB\"; is there bare \"Botafogo\"? Possibly. Alias bare → rj is right for Brazilian data.)\n  Wait — actually with alias \"botafogo\" → \"botafogo rj\" applied at team_key level, base \"botafogo\" never sees None state. States {rj, pb} → entities ✓.\n\nThis is the pattern: a small set of EXPLICIT state-granting aliases for bare ambiguous names (vitoria→ba, botafogo→rj, guarani→sp, america? not needed). I'll build the alias table carefully with a dev check listing all (base → states) to catch stragglers.\n\nAdditional FIFA club aliases needed (from FIFA club names):\n- \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico mg\"\n- \"atletico paranaense\" → \"atletico pr\"; \"athletico paranaense\" → \"atletico pr\" (BR-Football has BOTH \"Athletico Paranaense\" and \"Atletico Paranaense\"!)\n- \"atletico goianiense\" → \"atletico go\"\n- \"sport club do recife\" → \"sport pe\"; \"sport recife\" → \"sport pe\" (BR-Football)\n- \"ceara sporting club\" → \"ceara ce\"? → just \"ceara\" (states {ce, None} collapse anyway; alias to \"ceara ce\" also fine — collapse rule gives \"ceara\"... wait if alias gives \"ceara ce\" then base \"ceara\" states={ce, None} → |non_none|=1 → entity id \"ceara\" ✓ either way.)\n- \"america fc\" → \"america mg\" (FIFA's América FC (MG))\n- \"ec vitoria\"/\"vitoria ec\"/\"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\"\n- \"vasco da gama\" → \"vasco\"\n- \"america fc natal\" → \"america rn\"\n- \"fortaleza esporte clube\" → \"fortaleza\" (spec example name)\n- \"sport club corinthians paulista\" → \"corinthians\" (spec example)\n- \"clube de regatas do flamengo\" → \"flamengo\" (spec)\n- \"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\" (spec)\n- \"sao paulo fc\" → \"sao paulo\" (spec example \"São Paulo FC\")\n- \"gremio fbpa\"? skip.\n- \"ec bahia\" → \"bahia\"\n- \"chapecoense\" fine as-is.\n\nAlso country codes for Libertadores suffix strip: ARG BOL BRA CHI COL ECU EQU PAR PER URU VEN MEX USA CRC GUA HON SLV PAN JAM CAN TRI — I'll include a reasonable list.\n\nAlso handle \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\" — paren strip handles.\n\nEdge: Cup team \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → paren strip → \"boavista sport club - rj\" → state strip → \"boavista sport club\". Unique base ✓.\n\nNow dates:\n- Brasileirao: \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" → datetime\n- Cup: \"2012-03-07 16:00:00\"\n- Libertadores: same; skip \"NA\"\n- BR-Football: date \"2023-09-24\" + time \"20:00:00\"\n- novo: \"29/03/2003\" → %d/%m/%Y\n\nparse_date(s) handles: \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\", \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\". Return (date, time_str|None).\n\nGoals parsing: int where possible; BR-Football \"1.0\" → float→int; Libertadores has \"2\" strings and \"-\" for NA → skip \"-\".\n\n**Match dataclass fields**: date (date), time (str|None), competition (canonical), season (int), home, away (entity ids), home_display, away_display, home_goals, away_goals, round (int|None), stage (str|None), stadium (str|None), source (filename), corners/shots/attacks (int|None, int|None, int|None) — merged stats: home_corners, away_corners, home_shots, away_shots, home_attacks, away_attacks (only from BR-Football).\n\nHmm — keep it a dataclass with to_dict().\n\n**Player dataclass**: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, club_key (normalized entity-ish), position, position_group, jersey, height, weight, preferred_foot, value, wage, skills dict (the rating columns). to_dict() with core fields + full in detail.\n\nPlayer position group mapping:\nGK → goalkeeper\nLB LWB RB RWB CB LCB RCB → defender\nCDM LDM RDM CM LCM RCM CAM LAM RAM LM RM → midfielder\nLW RW LF RF LS RS ST CF → forward\n\n**Sorting stages for Libertadores \"bracket\"**: stage order map: group stage=0, round of 16=1, quarterfinals=2, semifinals=3, final=4.\n\n**Standings computation**:\n- matches from source-selected list for (competition, season).\n- Walk table: per team: pts, w, d, l, gf, ga. Sort by pts desc, wins desc?, gd, gf. (Brazilian tiebreak: wins, gd, gf.) \n- Return rows with position; champion = first; relegation zone = last 4 if 20 teams and competition Serie A.\n\n**team_rankings**: metric keys: points, wins, draws, losses, goals_for, goals_against, goal_diff, win_rate, home_wins, home_points, away_wins, away_points, home_win_rate, away_win_rate, matches. Computed over filtered match set (competition?, season?, team? no). Return top N rows.\n\n**stats_summary**: matches, goals, avg_goals, home_wins/draws/away_wins + rates, avg_home_goals/away_goals, biggest_win ref, teams count.\n\n**head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition?, season?, limit?)**: entity resolve both (must resolve to exactly 1 each; if ambiguous → error listing options), matches list, totals: wins_a, wins_b, draws, goals_a, goals_b; most recent match.\n\n**find_team(query)**: normalized substring match against entity id, display, variants; returns entities + match counts + per-competition counts. Limit 20.\n\n**search_matches** params:\n- team, opponent, home_team, away_team (str; entity-resolved, supports ambiguous→matches all entities with that base)\n- competition (canonical or alias), season (int), date_from/date_to (ISO), stage, round, venue (\"home\"/\"away\" — with team), limit (default 25, max 200), sort (\"date_desc\"|\"date_asc\")\n- source (exact filename or \"all\" — default curated)\nResponse: {count, total, matches:[...], teams_note?}\n\n**Now the MCP protocol module**:\n\n```python\nPROTOCOL_VERSIONS = {\"2024-11-05\", \"2025-03-26\", \"2025-06-18\"}\nclass MCPStdioServer:\n    def __init__(self, tool_registry, resources=None):\n    def run(stdin=sys.stdin, stdout=sys.stdout)\n```\n\nMessage handling:\n- parse line → json. If list → batch (handle each). \n- if \"method\" missing → invalid request error.\n- if \"id\" not present → notification: handle known notifications (initialized, cancelled) silently; unknown → ignore.\n- methods: initialize, ping, tools/list, tools/call, resources/list, resources/read, prompts/list, completion/complete? skip → method-not-found error for requests; for notifications ignore.\n- errors: {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":id,\"error\":{\"code\":..., \"message\":...}}.\n\nInitialize result:\n```python\n{\"protocolVersion\": client_version if in PROTOCOL_VERSIONS else \"2024-11-05\",\n \"capabilities\": {\"tools\": {\"listChanged\": False}, \"resources\": {\"listChanged\": False}, \"logging\": {}},\n \"serverInfo\": {\"name\": \"brazilian-soccer-mcp\", \"version\": __version__, \"title\"? no},\n \"instructions\": \"...\"}\n```\n\ntools/call: find tool; missing → tool not found error (or isError result? MCP spec: unknown tool → error response code -32602? Actually returning isError=true with message is acceptable; spec says invalid params error. I'll return JSON-RPC error -32602 \"Unknown tool\" — hmm, many servers return isError result. I'll use isError=true result for tool-level errors (including exceptions), and proper JSON-RPC errors for protocol-level issues.)\n\nArguments validation: light manual coercion (ints from strings, etc.) — write a small validator per tool using the declared schema types. Keep simple: coerce where possible, raise ValueError with message → isError result. \n\nFor tools/call response content: text = json.dumps(result, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2, default=str). \n\n**server.py**:\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"...\"\"\"\nimport argparse, json, sys\nfrom brazilian_soccer.repository import DataRepository\nfrom brazilian_soccer.tools import build_tool_registry\nfrom brazilian_soccer.protocol import MCPStdioServer\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    ap.add_argument(\"--data-dir\", default=<repo data/kaggle>)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--list-tools\", action=\"store_true\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--invoke\", metavar=\"TOOL\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--args\", default=\"{}\")\n    ...\n```\nstdio mode default; --invoke runs a tool once against a fresh repo and prints JSON to stdout (handy for smoke tests + the harness). --list-tools prints schemas.\n\nData dir default: `Path(__file__).parent / \"data\" / \"kaggle\"`. \n\n**Tests** (pytest, BDD GWT style). Structure every test as:\n```python\ndef test_...():\n    # Given ...\n    # When ...\n    # Then ...\n```\nwith descriptive names. Use module docstrings with Gherkin Feature/Scenario per the spec's testing approach. I'll include Gherkin-style docstrings at the top of each test module + GWT comments inside. That directly matches \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\" and the TASK's Gherkin examples.\n\nFixtures in conftest.py:\n```python\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef repo(): return DataRepository()  # loads once, ~1s\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef mcp_process():  # spawn subprocess, keep alive, helper to send messages\n```\nFor the process test: write a small helper class MCPClient that manages subprocess with text stdin/stdout, sends JSON lines, reads responses (with timeout). Test initialize → tools/list → tools/call search_matches → ping → unknown tool error → resources/list. Use sys.executable and cwd=repo root so relative paths work.\n\nCareful with subprocess buffering: use `bufsize=1` text mode, flush after write, and server flushes after each response. Also set env PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1.\n\nTimeout handling: read with select or rely on readline blocking with a watchdog thread? Simpler: run with `subprocess.Popen(..., text=True)` and readline() — if server misbehaves test hangs. Add a global timeout via `faulthandler`? Use pytest-timeout? Not installed. I'll implement read with select.select on POSIX (we're on darwin) with timeout → fail test on timeout. Fine.\n\nPerformance tests:\n- test_simple_lookup_under_2s: search_matches(team=\"flamengo\") — time it.\n- test_aggregate_under_5s: standings 2019 + team_rankings + head_to_head.\nAssert elapsed < 2.0 / 5.0. These should pass by 3+ orders of magnitude margin.\n\nKnown-fact tests (validate correctness of pipeline):\n- standings 2019 Serie A champion == \"flamengo\" entity display \"Flamengo\"; 2018 champion Palmeiras; 2015 Corinthians; 2021 Atlético-MG (entity \"atletico mg\"); 2022 Palmeiras; 2003 Cruzeiro.\n- 2019 relegated: CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fortaleza? Let me verify from my memory of 2019: relegated were CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fortaleza?? Hmm no — 2019 relegated: CSA (8th from bottom?)... I recall Cruzeiro relegated 2019 (yes, first time). CSA yes. Chapecoense yes. Fortaleza? NO — Fortaleza stayed (they survived on final day? 2019: Fortaleza finished 16th? hmm). Let me think again: 2019 bottom four: CSA (20th), Chapecoense (19th), Cruzeiro (17th?)... I'm not certain about the 4th (Avaí? — Avaí was relegated 2019! Yes Avaí finished 18th? Hmm). 2019 relegation: CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Avaí? I believe Avaí was relegated in 2019 (finished last-ish). Fortaleza survived. Let me just assert the champion + a couple of sure things, and check relegated via data when writing (I'll compute and eyeball; known-certain: Cruzeiro relegated 2019, CSA finished 20th). To be safe assert only champion + \"cruzeiro in relegated 2022\"? NO — Cruzeiro relegated 2019 AND played Serie B 2020-2022, promoted back 2022. Hmm.\n  I'll assert champions for several seasons (facts I'm sure of) and that relegated set for 2019 includes Cruzeiro and CSA (both certain). Actually is CSA 2019 certain? CSA finished 20th in 2019 with ~21 points. Yes I'm confident. Avaí 19th? Chapecoense 18th? hmm the exact set — assert only {cruzeiro, csa} ⊆ relegated. \n- head_to_head flamengo vs fluminense: matches > 50 across comps? Both in Serie A 2012-2022 (novo 2003-2011 Flamengo x Fluminense too). Assert count >= 30 and totals consistent (sum == count).\n- biggest wins: assert top margin