that counts? No — just cover them.\n\n**Coverage**: pytest-cov installed. I'll run with coverage to show good coverage. Not strictly needed but nice. I'll run pytest plain (fast) and optionally with --cov. Let me include coverage config in pyproject? No pyproject exists. I'll just run pytest; maybe add `pytest.ini`-style config file? Let me add a minimal `pytest.ini` with testpaths=tests and sane defaults (utf-8, verbose). Fine.\n\nWait — should there also be a way to run the server WITHOUT stdio for demo? The --invoke covers it.\n\nOne more consideration — **the JSON-RPC initialize protocolVersion negotiation**: respond with the version the client sent if we know it, else \"2024-11-05\". \n\n**logging**: to stderr only. logging.basicConfig(stream=sys.stderr, level=WARNING).\n\nNow, an important detail for `search_matches` default `limit`: the MCP tool should return bounded output (LLM context). Default 25, allow up to 500.\n\n**Output size for players**: default limit 25, max 200. Include total_count.\n\nAlright — also the **\"context block comment at the start of every code file\"** from prompts.txt — I decided: module docstrings. The user instruction in THIS session says \"implement everything TASK.md asks for\" — TASK.md says nothing about comments; system says no comments unless asked. Module docstrings = idiomatic documentation, I'll include rich ones (they double as the context block). Inline comments: only GWT markers in tests (that's BDD structure, arguably part of the testing approach the spec requests: \"Given/When/Then structured\"). The Gherkin scenarios in TASK.md's Testing Approach section justify GWT comments/docstrings in tests.\n\nLet me now also double-check the FIFA Neymar/Gabriel Barbosa values and actual biggest wins, and finalize alias decisions with a quick dry run of my normalization plan (prototype in a scratch script BEFORE writing the real modules — to validate entity resolution on real data). Actually, I'll write the real modules directly and then run a dev-check script; iterate on findings.\n\nOne more decision: **package name** — `brazilian_soccer`. Server name \"brazilian-soccer-mcp\".\n\nDirectory layout final:\n\n```\nserver.py\nbrazilian_soccer/__init__.py\nbrazilian_soccer/normalize.py\nbrazilian_soccer/models.py\nbrazilian_soccer/repository.py\nbrazilian_soccer/queries.py\nbrazilian_soccer/tools.py\nbrazilian_soccer/protocol.py\npytest.ini\ntests/__init__.py? (not needed with pytest.ini rootdir config; avoid __init__)\ntests/conftest.py\ntests/test_normalize.py\ntests/test_match_queries.py\ntests/test_team_queries.py\ntests/test_player_queries.py\ntests/test_competition_queries.py\ntests/test_statistics.py\ntests/test_mcp_protocol.py\ntests/test_performance.py\nREADME.md (update)\n```\n\nLet me write the code now. Start with normalize.py — the heart of data quality handling.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Team-name, date, and value normalization for heterogeneous Brazilian soccer datasets.\n...context block...\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nimport re\nimport unicodedata\nfrom datetime import date, datetime\n\nBRAZILIAN_STATES = frozenset({\n    \"ac\",\"al\",\"ap\",\"am\",\"ba\",\"ce\",\"df\",\"es\",\"go\",\"ma\",\"mt\",\"ms\",\"mg\",\"pa\",\n    \"pb\",\"pr\",\"pe\",\"pi\",\"rj\",\"rn\",\"rs\",\"ro\",\"rr\",\"sc\",\"sp\",\"se\",\"to\",\n})\n\nSOUTH_AMERICAN_COUNTRY_CODES = frozenset({\n    \"arg\",\"bol\",\"bra\",\"chi\",\"col\",\"ecu\",\"equ\",\"par\",\"per\",\"uru\",\"ven\",\n    \"mex\",\"usa\",\"crc\",\"gua\",\"hon\",\"slv\",\"pan\",\"jam\",\"can\",\"tri\",\n})\n\nFULL_NAME_ALIASES = {\n    # official / long names -> canonical \"base state\" form\n    \"sport club corinthians paulista\": \"corinthians\",\n    \"clube de regatas do flamengo\": \"flamengo\",\n    \"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\": \"palmeiras\",\n    \"sao paulo fc\": \"sao paulo\",\n    \"atletico mineiro\": \"atletico mg\",\n    \"clube atletico mineiro\": \"atletico mg\",\n    \"atletico paranaense\": \"atletico pr\",\n    \"athletico paranaense\": \"atletico pr\",\n    \"atletico goianiense\": \"atletico go\",\n    \"sport club do recife\": \"sport pe\",\n    \"sport recife\": \"sport pe\",\n    \"ceara sporting club\": \"ceara\",\n    \"america fc natal\": \"america rn\",\n    \"america fc\": \"america mg\",\n    \"ec vitoria\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"vitoria ec\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"vitoria\": \"vitoria ba\",   # hmm — WAIT this makes bare \"vitoria\" always BA... \n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm — \"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\" alias: but what about \"Vitória-ES\" bare appearing? \"Vitoria ES\" has explicit state. Bare \"Vitoria da Conquista\" is a different base. So bare \"vitoria\" → BA is right for this data (FIFA Vitória + novo Vitória are both BA). ✓ Keep.\n\nBut careful with alias application order and the state stripping: aliases produce strings like \"atletico mg\" which then go through state detection (trailing \"mg\") → base \"atletico\" state \"mg\". ✓ as designed.\n\nWhat about \"vasco da gama\" → \"vasco\": alias. And Brasileirao \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" → clean \"vasco da gama rj\" → state strip → \"vasco da gama\" → ALIAS... wait order: alias applied BEFORE state detection. \"vasco da gama rj\" doesn't match alias key \"vasco da gama\" (exact match). Then state strip → \"vasco da gama\" — alias not re-applied! I must apply aliases AFTER state stripping too, or use substring/endswith matching. \n\nBetter order:\n1. clean (accents, lowercase, parens, punctuation→space, collapse)\n2. strip trailing state UF → (base, state)\n3. strip trailing country code (for Libertadores \"-EQU\" → happens BEFORE UF? country codes are 3 letters, UFs are 2. Do country strip first (longer suffix first), then UF strip.\n4. apply aliases to the BASE (whole-string match): \"vasco da gama\" → \"vasco\" (and if alias target contains a state, re-strip? \"atletico mineiro\" → alias \"atletico mg\" → need second state-strip pass → final base \"atletico\" state \"mg\". So: after alias, re-run suffix detection on the aliased result, merging states (first non-None wins).)\n5. base canonical aliases: {\"athletico\": \"atletico\"}.\n\nImplementation:\n```python\ndef split_team(name: str) -> tuple[str, str | None]:\n    s = clean_name(name)               # step 1\n    state = None\n    s2, country = strip_country(s)     # e.g. \"olimpia par\" -> \"olimpia\", \"par\" (country) -> state stays None\n    s3, st = strip_state(s2)\n    state = st or state\n    base = s3\n    if base in FULL_NAME_ALIASES:\n        target = FULL_NAME_ALIASES[base]\n        t2, tst = strip_state(target)   # e.g. \"atletico mg\" -> \"atletico\",\"mg\"\n        base, state = t2, state or tst\n    base = BASE_ALIASES.get(base, base)\n    return base, state\n```\n\nWait — country strip: \"olimpia par\" — trailing token \"par\". Is \"par\" a country code? Yes (Paraguay). Strip → base \"olimpia\". But could \"par\" trailing be legit part of a club name? \"Atletico Par\"? no. OK.\n\nBut danger: 3-letter country codes on BRAZILIAN teams? \"Sao Paulo\" → \"sao paulo\" — trailing \"paulo\" no. Fine. What about a team like \"Atletico Tubarao\"? no. OK.\n\nAnother subtlety — strip_state: trailing token after hyphen/space that's a 2-letter UF: \"sport club do recife\" — trailing \"recife\"? no. \"America Mineiro\"? no. Fine. But \"Red Bull Bragantino SP\" → \"red bull bragantino\" ✓. \"Paysandu PA\"? ✓. BUT what about teams whose names END with a legit 2-letter word that is a UF code? E.g., \"CSA\" → cleaned \"csa\" single token no. \"Goiás EC\"? → \"goias ec\" → strip \"ec\"? \"ec\" is not UF ✓. Hmm \"Sport\" ok. \"Avai\" ok. What about \"Nautico Capibaribe\"? no. I think safe.\n\nBUT — \"Coritiba PR\" → \"coritiba\" ✓. \"Parana PR\" → \"parana\" ✓ — wait \"Paraná-PR\": clean → \"parana pr\" → strip state \"pr\" → \"parana\" ✓.\n\nWhat about Ceará \"Ceara CE\" → \"ceara\" ✓. Fortaleza \"Fortaleza CE\" → \"fortaleza\" ✓.\n\nNow, one potential bug: \"America MG\" → strip \"mg\" → \"america\" state mg ✓. \"America RN\" → \"america\" rn ✓. Both qualified later. \n\nAlso \"Botafogo PB\" → \"botafogo\" pb ✓.\n\nCountry code check must run BEFORE state (both can't apply). \"Guarani PAR\": country strip → \"guarani\" (country=PAR ignored for entity, it's not a Brazilian state). Hmm wait — should country code contribute to entity qualification? \"nacional uru\" vs \"nacional par\": country code IS the disambiguator! If I drop it entirely, both become base \"nacional\" state None → they'd MERGE into one entity \"nacional\" — wrong!\n\nUgh. Nacional (URU) and Nacional (PAR) — if both appear as bare \"nacional\"?? They don't — Libertadores always qualifies them: \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Nacional-URU\", \"Nacional (PAR)\", \"Nacional-PAR\". So instead of dropping the country code, TREAT it like a state qualifier: state slot = country code (lowercase). Entity id = base + \" \" + qualifier when ambiguous. \"nacional\" qualifiers {uru, par} → entities \"nacional uru\", \"nacional par\" ✓ distinct! And \"guarani\" qualifiers {par} + alias bare \"guarani\"→\"guarani sp\" (novo) → {sp, par} ✓.\n\nSo the \"state\" slot = {UF codes} ∪ {country codes}. Union set QUALIFIERS. Strip trailing token if in QUALIFIERS, remember it. \"olimpia par\" → base \"olimpia\" q \"par\" → entity \"olimpia par\" (unique? Olimpia-PR?? there's also \"Olimpia\" from ... no, only Paraguayan Olimpia here ✓).\n\nHmm wait — COLLISION DANGER: UF \"pr\" (Paraná) vs country... no country \"pr\". Country \"par\" vs UF... \"par\" is not a UF (PA is Pará, PR is Paraná). No overlap between 2-letter UFs and 3-letter country codes. And in the key I store the qualifier as-is (2 or 3 letters) — \"atletico pr\" (Paraná club) vs \"atletico par\"?? Would a Paraguayan \"Atlético\"-something appear? \"Atletico Paranaense\" is Brazilian... Fine.\n\nEdge: \"Barcelona-EQU\" → \"barcelona\" q \"equ\" → entity \"barcelona equ\"? qualifiers for base \"barcelona\" = {equ} only → |non_none|=1 → entity id = \"barcelona\" (collapse!). Wait my rule: if exactly one non-None qualifier → collapse to bare base. \"barcelona equ\" → \"barcelona\" ✓ good (no other Barcelonas).\n\nBut hold on — collapse rule with a single qualifier: \"flamengo\" {rj, None} → \"flamengo\" ✓. \"ceara\" {ce, None} → \"ceara\" ✓. Good — the collapse happens when |qualifiers| <= 1. When > 1 → qualified entity ids, and bare (None) variants → their own \"base\" entity (like guarani — except I alias bare guarani away).\n\nHmm — wait, actually for >1 qualifiers + None variants: \"botafogo\" {rj, pb, None?}: is there a bare \"Botafogo\" in match data? BR-Football? Let me handle via alias \"botafogo\"→\"botafogo rj\" anyway. And FIFA \"Botafogo\" bare. With alias, bare never reaches entity stage. OK.\n\nLet me define QUALIFIERS = UFs ∪ country codes, and stripping applies to the LAST whitespace/hyphen-separated token ONLY if the remainder is non-empty.\n\nEdge — \"Paraná\" bare: clean \"parana\" — trailing token \"parana\" not a qualifier ✓ stays \"parana\". BUT WAIT: \"Paraná\" vs \"Paraná-PR\" both exist (novo bare \"Paraná\", Brasileirao \"Parana-PR\") → qualifiers {pr, None} → collapse \"parana\" ✓ same club ✓. And FIFA \"Paraná\" joins ✓.\n\nNow — the alias for bare ambiguous bases. After loading, I'll print all bases with qualifier sets size > 1 AND bases with None among qualifiers, and eyeball. Add explicit state-granting aliases where needed (vitoria, botafogo, guarani...). This is the plan.\n\nOne more important case — **\"Sao Paulo\" state suffix vs club**: \"Sao Paulo-SP\" → \"sao paulo\" q sp → single → \"sao paulo\" ✓.\n\n**Cup \"América - MG\"**: clean → \"america mg\" → strip → \"america\" q mg ✓.\n\n**Libertadores Brazilian teams**: \"Atlético-MG\" → \"atletico\" q mg → entity \"atletico mg\" ✓ consistent with Brasileirao. \"Flamengo\" bare ✓.\n\n**FIFA \"Grêmio\"**: bare → base \"gremio\" q None; match data \"Gremio-RS\"/\"Grêmio\" → {rs, None} → collapse \"gremio\" ✓ joined.\n\nOK the design is solid. Now — the **entity assignment & resolution flow** in repository:\n\n```python\nclass TeamRegistry:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._variants: dict[entity_id, Counter[raw_name]] = defaultdict(Counter)\n        self._base_quals: dict[base, set[qual|None]] = defaultdict(set)\n        self._entities: dict[entity_id, TeamEntity]\n    def register(self, raw_name) -> entity_id\n    def resolve(self, query) -> list[TeamEntity]   # user-facing\n```\n\nBut there's a chicken-and-egg: entity ids depend on ALL data loaded (qualifier sets). Two-phase:\n- Phase 1: for every match row + player club, compute (base, qual) via split_team; collect raw variant per (base, qual).\n- Phase 2: compute base→quals; define entity_id(base, qual); group variants by entity id; build TeamEntity(display, key, variants, states).\n- Phase 3: assign entity ids to matches/players.\n\nPlayer clubs: register FIFA club names too (so entities include \"atlético mineiro\" mapping to atletico mg, and pure-FIFA clubs like \"FC Barcelona\" as their own entities — fine, they're just entities with 0 matches). resolve_team searches entities by key/display/variant substring; player queries filter players by club entity id OR normalized substring.\n\nHmm — do I want ALL 500+ FIFA clubs as \"entities\"? It's fine (they're just dict entries). find_team(\"barcelona\") would return FIFA's FC Barcelona (0 matches) + Libertadores Barcelona-EQU — with match counts shown. Nice actually.\n\nNow `resolve(query)` semantics:\n- clean query → split → (base, qual).\n- If qual given: entity = base+\" \"+qual if exists (after canonicalization); else fallback substring search.\n- If no qual: if base unique → that entity; if multiple → return all entities whose base == query base OR whose key starts with base? Also substring fallback for typos: e.g., \"palmeirass\"? Skip fuzzy — substring only.\n- Also substring search on raw variant names and display names (normalized): e.g., query \"corinthians\" → base exact. Query \"sport club corinthians\" → alias handles. Query \"barcelona\" → entity \"barcelona\" (EQU). Query \"fla\"? no — skip abbreviations. Maybe add a few: \"fla\" → flamengo? \"flu\" → fluminense? Cute but risky; skip.\n\nresolve returns list of TeamEntity; tools error helpfully when ambiguous (\"did you mean America-MG or America-RN?\").\n\n**CompetitionInfo**: canonical name, aliases, source preference per file, per-season match counts (curated), teams.\n\nWait, one more critical thing — **round field for Cup** is string round numbers \"1\"..\"8\"; parse int. Libertadores stage strings. Brasileirao round int. novo Rodada int. BR-Football: no round → None.\n\n**Half-time**: BR-Football ht_diff/at_diff? Those are per-team half-time goals? \"ht_diff: 0.0, at_diff: 0.0, ht_result: DRAW\" — probably home/away HT goals. I'll store as home_ht/away_ht. Not important.\n\nNow — **expected standings sanity**: 2021 Serie A from Brasileirao_Matches (380). 2021 BR-Football has 491 (bad) — skipped since Brasileirao preferred and complete. My rule: per (competition, season), order sources by preference; pick the FIRST source with any data. Hmm — \"first with any data\" → 2014: Brasileirao (380 complete) chosen over BR-Football (281) ✓. 2023: only BR-Football ✓. 2003-2011: only novo ✓. \n\nBut what if the preferred source is INCOMPLETE for a season where a lower source is complete? E.g., hypothetical: Brasileirao missing 2016 (it's not). In our data: Brasileirao always 380 for 2012-2022 ✓; novo always 380+ for 2003-2019 ✓; Cup: Brazilian_Cup covers 2012-2021 fully? 1337 matches over 10 seasons... some seasons may be partial (2012: how many? whatever). BR-Football Copa 2014-2023. For Cup seasons 2014-2021 Brazilian_Cup preferred (has rounds); 2022-2023 BR-Football. Partial coverage risk accepted; standings for Cup don't make sense anyway (knockout). \n\nRule refinement: pick first source with >= 50% of the max rows that source-season has? Overkill. Keep \"first source with data\". Actually hmm — one risk: if Brazilian_Cup were missing season 2015 entirely, BR-Football fills. Fine.\n\nAlso Libertadores never overlaps.\n\nNow — hmm, wait. One more thought on \"curated\" match list for general search/stats: using per-season single-source for Serie A means BR-Football Serie B/C are included fully (no overlap), Libertadores fully, Cup from Brazilian_Cup file + BR-Football 2022-23. Total curated ≈ 4180 + (novo 2003-2011: 552+552+462+380×6=3896... let me compute: 2003:552, 2004:552, 2005:462, 2006-2011: 380×6=2280 → total 2003-2011 = 552+552+462+2280 = 3846) + Cup 1337 + Lib 1254 + BR-Football (Serie B 3677 + Serie C 1807 + Copa 2022-23 + Serie A 2023 377+...). ≈ 4180+3846+1337+1254+3677+1807+~300+377 ≈ 16,778. Plus extended-stats merges. OK.\n\n**head-to-head \"in dataset\" counts** will now be per curated set — consistent, deduped. \n\nAlright — also **`total_corners`** etc: store corners/shots/attacks per match when available (from BR-Football or merged). In search output include when present.\n\nLet me also decide **sorting of matches**: (date, time, competition). \n\n**Now write everything.** I'll be careful about:\n- No external deps (stdlib only).\n- UTF-8 everywhere: open files with encoding=\"utf-8\"; json.dumps ensure_ascii=False.\n- Logging to stderr.\n\nLet me write `normalize.py` first.\n\nAlso — positions: FIFA 'Position' may be empty for some players? Handle: position \"\" → group \"unknown\". \n\nPlayer skills parsing: values like \"88+2\" — keep raw string in skills dict; also parse int prefix where digits. For queries maybe not needed; just expose.\n\nfifa columns: the full column list is long; I'll map a subset of skill columns explicitly: the standard FIFA 19 columns from LS..GKReflexes + Crossing..SlidingTackle. I'll collect ALL columns not otherwise consumed as \"attributes\" dict — simplest: attributes = {k: v for k, v in row.items() if k not in CORE_FIELDS and v not in (\"\", None)}. That auto-adapts.\n\nLet me write the code files now. I'll write