corinthians\" (collapse since {sp, None} single) ✓. Or target \"corinthians\" bare → base \"corinthians\" qual None ✓ same entity either way. I'll write targets that carry the qualifier when known (more informative).\n\nFull list:\n\n```python\nFULL_NAME_ALIASES = {\n    \"sport club corinthians paulista\": \"corinthians sp\",\n    \"clube de regatas do flamengo\": \"flamengo rj\",\n    \"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\": \"palmeiras sp\",\n    \"sao paulo fc\": \"sao paulo sp\",\n    \"atletico mineiro\": \"atletico mg\",\n    \"clube atletico mineiro\": \"atletico mg\",\n    \"atletico paranaense\": \"atletico pr\",\n    \"athletico paranaense\": \"atletico pr\",\n    \"atletico goianiense\": \"atletico go\",\n    \"sport club do recife\": \"sport pe\",\n    \"sport recife\": \"sport pe\",\n    \"ceara sporting club\": \"ceara ce\",\n    \"fortaleza esporte clube\": \"fortaleza ce\",\n    \"ec bahia\": \"bahia ba\",\n    \"esporte clube bahia\": \"bahia ba\",\n    \"ec vitoria\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"vitoria ec\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"vitoria\": \"vitoria ba\",\n    \"botafogo\": \"botafogo rj\",\n    \"botafogo fr\": \"botafogo rj\",\n    \"guarani\": \"guarani sp\",\n    \"america fc\": \"america mg\",\n    \"america fc natal\": \"america rn\",\n    \"gremio fbpa\": \"gremio rs\",\n    \"sc internacional\": \"internacional rs\",\n    \"vasco da gama rj\": \"vasco rj\",  # hmm... or BASE alias\n    \"santos fc\": \"santos sp\",\n    \"fluminense fc\": \"fluminense rj\",\n}\n```\n\nWAIT — \"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\": full-string match on cleaned \"vitoria\". But BR-Football \"Vitoria ES\" cleaned \"vitoria es\" ≠ \"vitoria\" ✓ safe. novo \"Vitória\" → \"vitoria\" → alias → \"vitoria ba\" ✓. FIFA \"Vitória\" ✓. BUT — hmm, Libertadores? \"Vitória\"? not there. OK.\n\n\"botafogo\" → \"botafogo rj\": cleaned \"botafogo\" only bare FIFA/novo... wait novo has \"Botafogo-RJ\" (suffixed) ✓ not aliased. Any BARE \"Botafogo\" in match data meaning RJ ✓ correct anyway.\n\n\"guarani\" → \"guarani sp\": bare only in novo ✓.\n\n\"vasco da gama rj\" — hmm, alias with target \"vasco rj\"? Or BASE_ALIASES {\"vasco da gama\": \"vasco\"}. Both work; BASE approach also handles hypothetical \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\"→\"vasco da gama\"+rj→ base alias → \"vasco\"+rj = \"vasco rj\" — and novo bare \"Vasco\" → \"vasco\" (qual None) — entity: base \"vasco\" quals {rj, None} → collapse \"vasco\" ✓ all join. Use BASE alias.\n\nBASE_ALIASES = {\"athletico\": \"atletico\", \"vasco da gama\": \"vasco\", \"america fc\": \"america\"}\n\nAlso maybe \"gremio osasco audax\"? nah.\n\nAlso — **display names**: entity display = most common RAW variant. For \"flamengo\": raws \"Flamengo-RJ\" (Brasileirao ~380), \"Flamengo\" (Lib+novo+BRF ~200)... → \"Flamengo-RJ\". Eh — display \"Flamengo-RJ\" acceptable? The spec examples display \"Flamengo\". Hmm — I'll prefer the SHORT display: choose the most common raw with the fewest characters? Or strip qualifier from display? Let me pick: among variants, prefer the one equal to title-cased(base) if present... Simple heuristic: pick most common; if a strictly shorter variant exists with count >= 20% of the top, prefer the shorter. Meh. Cleaner: display = most common raw variant — but for entity keys that collapsed (single qual), strip the trailing qualifier from display when the raw is \"Name-UF\" form → \"Flamengo-RJ\" → \"Flamengo\". That's cosmetic and nice. For qualified entities (america mg) keep a suffixed display (reconstruct \"América-MG\"? from variants like \"America MG\"/\"América-MG\" pick most common then normalize to \"Base-UF\" casing? overkill — just use most common raw; \"America MG\" fine).\n\nDecision: display = most common raw; if entity was collapsed (single qualifier) then strip a trailing \"-XX\"/\" - XX\"/\" XX\" qualifier from the display when present. Good enough.\n\nActually — keep it simple and predictable: display = most common raw variant, period. Tests won't depend on display much. Hmm, but answers read \"Flamengo-RJ 2-1 Fluminense-RJ\" — spec example shows \"Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense\". Strip suffix for collapsed entities. I'll implement `_pretty_display`.\n\nOK — now **queries.py** functions all take `repo` + params, return plain dicts. And **tools.py** builds JSON-Schemas + wraps. Let me now write the actual code. I'll write files with the write tool.\n\nLet me also decide numbers: \n- JSON tool schema format: {\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{...},\"required\":[...]}.\n- Tool descriptions with usage examples (LLM-facing).\n\nTime to write. After code, dev-check script:\n1. load repo, print load_report\n2. print base→quals ambiguous cases (eyeball)\n3. standings 2015/2018/2019/2021/2022/2003 champions\n4. flamengo-fluminense h2h count\n5. biggest wins top 5\n6. FIFA Brazilian clubs joined entities + counts\n7. Gabriel Barbosa lookup\n8. 2023 palmeiras matches (cross-file: copa 2023 + serie a 2023)\n\nThen fix issues, then write tests, run pytest, run MCP smoke via --invoke and a real subprocess e2e, update README.\n\nLet me write normalize.py now.\n\nActually one more thing — date formats in BR-Football: \"2023-09-24\" — any weird ones? \"2014-05-17\"? fine. And Cup/Brasileirao datetimes \"2012-03-07 16:00:00\". Libertadores NA handled. novo \"29/03/2003\". OK.\n\nAnd **time** field: for novo no time (None). Match.time = \"18:30\" string when present.\n\n**Sort stability**: sort_key = (date, time or \"00:00\", sequence). I'll add an internal `_seq` for stable sort of same-day matches? Just sort by (date, time or \"\"). Fine.\n\nWriting code now. normalize.py:","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b42639b001AgVOoKqppHWhGl","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","messageID":"msg_05b3a764f001xzOaSPbYHFCZWb"}}
