nue. So player filtered out unless club_key in club_keys. OK actually correct: with entities found, only club_key matching passes. When no entities → club_keys empty set → `if club_keys:` false → elif club_substring handles substring. But the inner nested condition is dead code since club_substring is None when club_keys non-empty... Actually wait: `club_substring` is set ONLY when entities empty. So in `if club_keys:` branch, club_substring is always None → simplify:\n\n```python\nif club_keys and player.club_key not in club_keys:\n    continue\nif club_substring and club_substring not in clean_text(player.club):\n    continue\n```\nMuch cleaner. Fix.\n\n2. `stats_summary` date_range uses pool[0]/pool[-1] — but pool isn't sorted! `_apply_filters` preserves repo.matches order which IS sorted by sort_key (curated sorted). OK — but pool[0] is min date ✓ since repo.matches sorted ascending. Fine but fragile; use min/max explicitly.\n\n3. In `standings`, `relegated` logic: `position > teams_count - 4` → for 20 teams: positions 17,18,19,20 ✓ bottom 4.\n\n4. `head_to_head` — filter pool by competition/season via _apply_filters ✓.\n\n5. `_apply_filters` season handling: `season` param may be None or is_nothing → skip; else parse. But search_matches passes season=None → fine.\n\n6. `search_matches` — `round` shadows builtin `round` as parameter name... it's fine within the function (no use of builtin round there). But I named the parameter `round` — in the signature `round=None`. Inside I do `round_value = parse_int(round_no)` — wait I pass `round_no=round` ✓. OK but shadowing builtin is ugly; keep (matches tool schema name \"round\").\n\nHmm wait — actually in search_matches I have `round=None` param and inside I never call builtin round ✓. In `standings`/`stats_summary` I call `round(...)` builtin — different function ✓ fine.\n\n7. `team_stats` — `_canonical_competition(repo, competition)` returns None for None ✓.\n\n8. `derby_matches` — DERBIES pairs use entity keys: \"botafogo rj\", \"vitoria ba\", \"guarani sp\", \"atletico mg\", \"atletico pr\" — I need to make sure these match actual entity keys produced by the registry:\n   - \"flamengo\" ✓ collapsed\n   - \"vasco\" ✓ (base alias)\n   - \"sao paulo\" ✓\n   - \"gremio\" ✓, \"internacional\" ✓\n   - \"atletico mg\" — qualified (3 atleticos) ✓\n   - \"cruzeiro\" ✓\n   - \"botafogo rj\" ✓ qualified (pb exists)\n   - \"bahia\" ✓ collapsed\n   - \"vitoria ba\" — qualified {ba, es} ✓\n   - \"ceara\" ✓, \"fortaleza\" ✓\n   - \"atletico pr\" ✓, \"coritiba\" ✓\n   - \"sport\" ✓, \"nautico\" ✓\n   - \"avai\" ✓, \"figueirense\" ✓\n   - \"ponte preta\" ✓, \"guarani sp\" ✓ (alias makes novo Guarani → \"guarani sp\"; states {sp, par} → qualified ✓)\n\n9. `player_detail` — \"Gabriel Barbosa\" query: clean_text both sides substring ✓.\n\n10. In `search_players` nationality: exact then substring — my implementation:\n```python\nif clean_text(player.nationality) != nationality_key:\n    if nationality_key not in clean_text(player.nationality):\n        continue\n```\nWeird logic: if not equal AND not substring → skip. If equal → pass. If substring → pass. So effectively substring match ✓ but written confusingly. Simplify: `if nationality_key not in clean_text(player.nationality): continue`. \"brazil\" in \"brazil\" ✓. Also \"Brazil\" clean → \"brazil\" ✓.\n\n11. `find_team` iterates all players per entity — 18k × entities... fine (few entities).\n\nAlso `_PLAYER... sort by name` etc fine.\n\n12. `biggest_wins` `_winner_display` helper defined AFTER biggest_wins uses it — fine at runtime (module-level def order doesn't matter for call time).\n\n13. `stats_summary` — `pool[0]`/`pool[-1]` after filtering: matches preserve repo.matches sorted order ✓ but to be safe use min/max by date.\n\nLet me fix these small issues now with edits. Then write tools.py + protocol.py + server.py.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b4970b8001kgqcCzod1erZYG","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","messageID":"msg_05b490fb0001W7kbnggihCG0XV"}}
