ivate... Let me restructure: I'll import it and catch (QueryError, _InvalidParams). Actually simpler to make the invoke path go through the same code. Let me adjust:\n\n```python\nfrom brazilian_soccer.protocol import MCPStdioServer, build_dataset_resources\n...\ntry:\n    result = server._tools_call(...)\nexcept QueryError as error:  # can't happen — _tools_call catches QueryError internally\nexcept _InvalidParams as error:  # unknown tool / bad params\n```\n\n_tools_call catches QueryError itself and returns isError result. It raises _InvalidParams for unknown tool or bad args. So in main I need to catch _InvalidParams. Let me just catch `Exception` broadly with message. Or import the class. Import private is ugly but fine within the same project. Let me restructure to catch both:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    result = server._tools_call({\"name\": options.invoke, \"arguments\": arguments})\nexcept Exception as error:\n    print(json.dumps({\"error\": str(error)}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))\n    return 1\n```\n\nAnd if result has isError → still print (it's a JSON error body) and return 0? Return 1 to signal failure. Let me handle: check result.get(\"isError\").\n\nLet me update server.py accordingly. Also `server._tools_list()` private use — acceptable for this helper CLI, but cleaner to expose public helpers. I'll add public methods? The class has _tools_list/_tools_call as dispatch helpers. For the CLI I'll just use them; it's fine within the same codebase. Actually, let me keep it clean: catch Exception around _tools_call and handle","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b4ae9b3001Wh1V7QWFUPSKT4","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","messageID":"msg_05b4ab581001HbJI1Ebesrwnli"}}
