}\n \n+PREFIX_TOKENS = frozenset({\"se\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"aa\"})\n+\n _PAREN_RE = re.compile(r\"\\(([^)]*)\\)\")\n _PUNCT_RE = re.compile(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\")\n _SPACE_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s+\")\n \n","additions":2,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer/normalize.py","time":{"start":1788238469503,"end":1788238469505}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Issues remaining:\n\n1. **`resolve 'SE Palmeiras' -> []`** — \"SE Palmeiras\" cleaned → \"se palmeiras\" → trailing \"palmeiras\" not a qualifier → base \"se palmeiras\" — no match! I should handle common club-name prefixes: \"SE\" (Sociedade Esportiva), \"AA\", \"EC\", \"SC\" etc. Also note \"América de Cali\" and \"América de Natal\" matched substring \"america\" — because resolve's substring fallback over keys matched \"america de cali\". For \"america\" the same-base step should have caught only \"america mg\"/\"america rn\"... but it ALSO returned \"america de cali\"/\"america de natal\" — because same_base check `k == base or k.startswith(base + \" \")` matches \"america de cali\" too! Hmm — \"america de cali\" startswith \"america \" ✓ → included in same_base. Is that desirable? \"America\" → the Colombian América de Cali?? For the Brazilian-soccer context, when user says \"America\" they mean the Brazilian clubs; but including foreign same-prefix clubs in the disambiguation list is arguably helpful... but noisy. Hmm. The same_base step should really be \"entities whose BASE equals the query base\" — i.e., keys that are exactly base or base + single qualifier token. \"america de cali\" is base \"america de cali\" ≠ \"america\". Let me tighten: same_base = keys where key == base or (key starts with base + \" \" AND the remainder is a qualifier code — i.e., key.rsplit(\" \", 1)[-1] in QUALIFIER_ABBRS). That distinguishes \"america mg\" (qualifier) from \"america de cali\" (different base). \n\n   For the \"se palmeiras\" prefix case: add common prefix tokens to strip: tokens like \"se\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"aa\", \"ac\", \"ad\" (prefix forms)? Hmm — risky to strip \"sc\" from \"sc internacional\"... I have full-string alias for that. Let me add a small PREFIX set removal for tokens in {\"se\", \"ec\", \"aa\"}? \"SE Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\" ✓. \"EC Bahia\" → \"bahia\" ✓ (also aliased). \"AA Ponte Preta\" → \"ponte preta\" ✓. What about \"SC\" — \"SC Internacional\" aliased explicitly; \"SC Corinthians\" → \"corinthians\"? \"SC\" prefix appears in \"SC Corinthians Paulista\" (common name!). The spec example is \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" (aliased). Adding \"sc\" to prefix strip: any false positives? \"SC\" as a whole prefix in Brazilian club names = \"Sport Club\" ✓ safe. Add {\"se\",\"ec\",\"sc\",\"aa\",\"ac\",\"adc\",\"gt\"}? Hmm \"ac\" — \"AC\" could appear? Let me keep a conservative set: {\"se\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"aa\"}. Also PREFIX stripping must not break single-token names — only strip when >1 token remains.\n\n   Wait — careful: \"America de Natal\" — no prefix issue. What about \"Santa Cruz\" — \"Santa\" is not a prefix to strip ✓. \"São Paulo\" — \"sao\" not stripped ✓ (not in set). OK.\n\n2. **team_rankings metric \"away points\"** — clean_text turns underscores into spaces (\"away_points\" → \"away points\")! My clean_text replaces non-alphanumeric with space — so metric with underscore breaks. Fix: don't clean_text the metric; normalize manually: strip, lower, replace spaces/hyphens with underscores. Same for `sort` in search_players (clean_text(\"overall\") fine but underscores would break — my enum values have no underscores except... \"date_desc\"/\"date_asc\" in search_matches sort! I don't clean_text that one — I compare `sort != \"date_asc\"` raw ✓ but let me normalize consistently).\n\n   Fix: helper `_norm_key(value)` = value.strip().lower().replace(\"-\", \"_\").replace(\" \", \"_\") then use for metric/sort.\n\n3. **\"America\" resolution** — after tightening same_base, \"america\" → [america mg, america rn] ✓ good (Colombian excluded).\n\n4. Palmeiras 2023: 43 matches (Serie A 37 from BR-Football partial + Copa 6) ✓ cross-file works.\n\n5. Derbies 2023: 16 ✓.\n\n6. Copa do Brasil total: 1554 (1321 + BR-Football 2022-2023 233) ✓.\n\n7. Grêmio display \"Gremio\" — picked bare variant ✓ acceptable (BR-Football unaccented \"Gremio\" most common). Fine.\n\n8. Also note resolve('Grêmio') works ✓.\n\nLet me also double-check \"SE Palmeiras\" after prefix fix → base \"palmeiras\" ✓.\n\nAnd \"América de Natal\" (RN club full name) — appears in BR-Football? \"America de Natal\" → base \"america de natal\" — distinct from \"america rn\"! Should alias \"america de natal\" → \"america rn\". And \"america fc natal\" aliased already. Add \"america de natal\" → \"america rn\". Hmm — and \"América de Cali\" stays foreign ✓.\n\nLet me apply these fixes:\n\n1. normalize.py: PREFIX_TOKENS = {\"se\",\"ec\",\"sc\",\"aa\"} stripped from start (when more than one token remains). Plus alias \"america de natal\" → \"america rn\".\n2. repository.py resolve(): tighten same_base to qualifier-only suffixes.\n3. queries.py: _norm_key for metric + sort params.\n\nFor prefix stripping in clean_text? No — better in split_team after alias lookup (aliases use full strings). Order: cleaned = clean_text → prefix strip → alias lookup → qualifier split → base alias. Hmm — if I prefix-strip before alias, \"EC Bahia\" → \"bahia\" ✓; \"SC Internacional\" → \"internacional\" → then alias \"internacional\" → \"internacional rs\" ✓ (my full alias already handles bare internacional). \"AA Ponte Preta\" → \"ponte preta\" ✓.\n\nWhere exactly? In split_team:\n```python\ncleaned = clean_text(name)\ntokens = cleaned.split(\" \")\nif len(tokens) > 1 and tokens[0] in PREFIX_TOKENS:\n    cleaned = \" \".join(tokens[1:])\ncleaned = FULL_NAME_ALIASES.get(cleaned, cleaned)\n```\nWait — but \"SE Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\" ✓. Careful: does any real club name START with these tokens as integral? \"SE\" = \"Sociedade Esportiva\" ✓ prefix. \"EC\" ✓. \"SC\" ✓. \"AA\" ✓ (Associação Atlética). Hmm — \"SC\" though... \"sc corinthians\" → \"corinthians\" ✓. OK safe.\n\nHmm — one more: \"AA\" in tokens[0] — could a name be \"AA ...\" where AA matters? \"AA Ponte Preta\" → ponte preta ✓ desired. OK.\n\nEdge: single token \"SE\" alone → len==1 → no strip ✓.\n\nNow fix all three files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b518d4c001hr1S3quA15Awad","sessionID":"ses_fa4ccb140ffeDOyFhEFIFiRBUT","messageID":"msg_05b50f688001ELnxZIqgspQPCz"}}
