tical either way.\n+\"palmeiras\": \"palmeiras sp\",\n+\"corinthians\": \"corinthians sp\",\n+\"sao paulo\": \"sao paulo sp\",\n+\"gremio\": \"gremio rs\",\n+\"cruzeiro\": \"cruzeiro mg\",\n+\"vasco\": \"vasco rj\",\n+\"bahia\": \"bahia ba\",\n+\"ceara\": \"ceara ce\",\n+\"fortaleza\": \"fortaleza ce\",\n+\"chapecoense\": \"chapecoense sc\",\n+\"coritiba\": \"coritiba pr\",\n+\"criciuma\": \"criciuma sc\",\n+\"figueirense\": \"figueirense sc\",\n+\"avai\": \"avai sc\",\n+\"goias\": \"goias go\",\n+\"sport\": \"sport pe\",\n+\"parana\": \"parana pr\",\n+\"ponte preta\": \"ponte preta sp\",\n+\"joinville\": \"joinville sc\",\n+\"csa\": \"csa al\",\n+\"cuiaba\": \"cuiaba mt\",\n # Renamed club: Bragantino became Red Bull Bragantino in 2019.\n \"bragantino\": \"red bull bragantino\",\n \"bragantino sp\": \"red bull bragantino\",\n # Libertadores spells Athletico-PR without any suffix.\n \"athletico\": \"atletico pr\",\n-\"atletico\": \"atletico pr\",\n \"america fc\": \"america mg\",\n \"america fc natal\": \"america rn\",\n \"america de natal\": \"america rn\",\n \"america rj\": \"america rj\",\n","additions":23,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer/normalize.py","time":{"start":1788239074226,"end":1788239074227}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Clear picture now:\n\n1. **Santos forwards**: exactly 3 players — my assertion `> 3` should be `>= 3` (data has 3 Santos forwards). Fix test.\n\n2. **player_detail by id 158023 = L. Messi** — as suspected; fix test to expect \"L. Messi\".\n\n3. **Real Madrid IS a FIFA club entity** — so search_matches(team=\"Real Madrid\") resolves to an entity with 0 matches → returns empty results, no error. The right test: use a truly unknown name. Fix test to \"Bora Bora United\".\n\n4. **away_points: Flamengo 486, Cruzeiro 486** — tied! The ranking puts Flamengo first (tiebreak: points, then goal_diff). São Paulo not even in top 3?! São Paulo has 918 matches — São Paulo's away points must be < 459. Wait — São Paulo 918 matches vs Flamengo 950. Flamengo has more matches (Libertadores runs + 2023 partial Serie A + Copa). So Flamengo legitimately tops away points all-time in this dataset. My test expectation \"Sao Paulo leads\" was based on the earlier (entity-split-bugged?) run — no wait, the earlier run ALSO had the merge fix... Hmm, earlier: \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\" — that was BEFORE the re-expansion-block removal? No wait — that dev run output was from the run AFTER alias fix. Flamengo 384 then vs 486 now?! And São Paulo 387 then vs <459 now?? Numbers changed a LOT.\n\nHmm — what changed between those runs? The re-expansion block removal (Bragantino fix). Let me think about what the OLD block did to (\"flamengo\",\"rj\") pairs: \"Flamengo-RJ\" → base \"flamengo\", qual \"rj\" → OLD: base \"flamengo\" in FULL ✓ → expand \"flamengo rj\" → split → (\"flamengo\",\"rj\") → qualifier = \"rj\" or \"rj\" — same. So no change... What about (\"santos\",\"sp\")? same pattern ✓ no change.\n\nHmm, so why did the numbers change?? Let me recheck — maybe I misread which dev run produced 387. Timeline:\n- Run A (after alias fix): \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\" — hmm wait, was this BEFORE or AFTER removing the re-expansion? Looking at my message order: I ran dev_check (2nd time, full output) → it had the \"duplicates_shadowed: 3899\" and showed away ranking São Paulo 387 — that was with the RE-EXPANSION BLOCK STILL PRESENT. THEN I removed the re-expansion block (Bragantino fix) and ran tail — which didn't show the ranking (crashed at Gabriel before it? No wait — the tail run showed \"Best away (points)\"? Let me check... the third run tail-30 output ended with \"2018 Serie A matches with corners: 294 / 380\" — no, it didn't include the away ranking since it came AFTER Gabriel Jesus... hmm the tail -30 output DID show \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387)...\" — no it didn't. Let me look... The tail -30 showed: \"...Palmeiras 2023: 43 matches...Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]...2019 Serie A matches with stadium: 378\". YES it DID show São Paulo 387 — that was AFTER the re-expansion removal. So between that state and NOW, away_points for Flamengo jumped 384 → 486 and São Paulo dropped below 459?? That's a huge change — something big changed. What have I changed since? (1) PREFIX_TOKENS, (2) resolve tightening, (3) metric _norm_key fix, (4) \"america de natal\" alias.\n\n**THE METRIC FIX!** Before, `clean_text(\"away_points\")` → \"away points\" → NOT in _RANKING_METRICS → QueryError... no wait, the dev run showed results. Hmm — team_rankings(repo, metric=\"away_points\") in dev_check — with the clean_text bug it would have raised \"Unknown metric 'away points'\"... and indeed THE LAST DEV RUN (before fixes) DID raise that error! I saw: \"QueryError: Unknown metric 'away points'\" at the end of the full run. So the 387/384 numbers came from the SECOND run — wait no, the second run ALSO would have had the same bug (clean_text was there from the start)... Hmm! Let me re-examine: the \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387)...\" line appeared in the SECOND dev run output (the one with full JSON report)? Looking back at my outputs:\n\n- 2nd dev run (after first alias fix): ends with \"QueryError: Unknown metric 'away points'\" — NO away ranking shown! Right — the run crashed at Gabriel Barbosa before... no wait, it crashed at Gabriel (line 61) which is BEFORE the away ranking (line 96). So where did I see \"São Paulo 387\"? That was in the run BEFORE... the FIRST run? No — first run crashed at Flamengo h2h?? No — first run crashed at int('1.0')... Let me look again at my history: \n\n- Run 1: crash int('1.0').\n- Run 2 (after int fix): full output ending with \"Traceback ... No player matches 'Gabriel Barbosa'\" — and it showed \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\"?? Hmm — no! Run 2's output ended at the Gabriel crash — the away ranking line comes AFTER Gabriel in dev_check.py... Actually NO: in run 2, I saw at the very end before the traceback: nothing about away. But in run 2 output I DO see... let me scan: \"Palmeiras 2023: 43...\" no. Run 2 output ended: \"...Flamengo players: 0 / resolve ... / Derbies 2023: 16 / competitions / Palmeiras 2023 / Traceback at away ranking (Unknown metric 'away points')\". YES — run 2 ended with the away_points metric ERROR. So \"São Paulo 387\" NEVER appeared in any dev run! I hallucinated it from... the \"Best away (points)\" line in run 2? Run 2 output DID show \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\"?? Scanning run 2 output above... I see in run 2: \"Traceback ... line 96, in <module> tr = queries.team_rankings(...)... QueryError: Unknown metric 'away points'\". And BEFORE that: \"Palmeiras 2023: 43 matches {...}\". Hmm — no São Paulo line. Where did I get 387? In run 3 (after re-expansion removal + prefix fixes): \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\" — hmm did run 3 show that? Run 3's output (tail -50) ended with \"2019 Serie A matches with stadium: 378 / 380, 2018 corners 294/380\"... and it DID include \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\"? Scanning my run 3 output above: yes! Run 3 (tail -50) shows \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\". Hmm — so in run 3 the metric WORKED (I had fixed _norm_key by then) and São Paulo was 387. NOW São Paulo is below 459?! And Flamengo 384 → 486?!\n\nWait — that CAN'T be from my recent edits (only PREFIX, resolve, metrics, alias-america). UNLESS — the metric fix changed semantics! Before _norm_key, `clean_text(\"away_points\")` = \"away points\" → raised error in run 2. In run 3, I had ALREADY applied the _norm_key fix? Let me check order: run 3 was AFTER \"removing re-expansion + updating Gabriel Jesus in dev_check\" — hmm no. Let me retrace:\n\nEdit sequence after run 2:\na. Removed re-expansion block.\nb. Updated dev_check (Gabriel Jesus).\nc. Run 3 (tail -30): showed Fla-Flu 44 18-12-14, Gabriel Jesus OK, resolves OK, derbies 16, Palmeiras 2023, then **QueryError: Unknown metric 'away points'** — the tail -30 ended with that traceback! YES — run 3 ALSO ended with the metric error.\n\nSo where did \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ...]\" come from?? It was in the FOURTH run — the one where I checked Corinthians/Lib finals/etc. (the big exploration script). YES — that run had `queries.team_rankings(repo, metric=\"away_points\")`... no wait, that script's last line was rankings home win rate... hmm, it printed \"2023 wins: [('Gremio', 21)...\". Hmm no São Paulo 387 there either!!\n\nOK — I definitely saw \"Best away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\" SOMEWHERE... It was run 3's full output? The tail -30 showed: \"...Palmeiras 2023: 43 matches {'Brasileirão Serie A': 37, 'Copa do Brasil': 6}\\nBest away (points): [('Sao Paulo', 387), ('Flamengo', 384), ('Palmeiras', 348)]\\n\\n2019 Serie A matches with stadium: 378 / 380...\" — YES that's run 3's tail! But run 3 ALSO had the metric error?? No — the metric error was run 3's END. Both can't be true... unless run 3 had BOTH — the tail -50 I saw showed the away ranking AND then the stadium/corners lines — no metric error. And the METRIC error was run... 2! Yes — run 2 ended with metric error; run 3 (after _norm_key fix? NO — I applied _norm_key AFTER run 3...).\n\nUgh, I'm confusing myself. Let me just settle it EMPIRICALLY: the current numbers are Flamengo 486, Cruzeiro 486, Palmeiras 459 — with 950/933/878 matches. The OLD \"São Paulo 387\" reading — whether or not I hallucinated it — matters only for the test. Question: are CURRENT numbers correct?\n\nSanity: Flamengo 950 matches (2003-2023, Serie A ~760 + Libertadores ~100 + Copa ~80 ≈ 940-950 ✓). Away points 486 over ~475 away matches ≈ 1.02 pts/away-match — plausible for Flamengo.\n\nSão Paulo: 918 matches, away points must be ~450ish. With tie Flamengo 486 = Cruzeiro 486.\n\nHmm wait — but why would São Paulo have FEWER away points than Cruzeiro/Palmeiras? São Paulo 2003-2023: strong but... Cruzeiro 2003-2019 strong era (won 2003)... plausible. Palmeiras 878 matches — fewer than São Paulo's 918? Palmeiras played Serie B 2012-2013 (missed Serie A seasons but played Serie B which counts in \"all matches\"!). Hmm wait — team_rankings with no competition filter includes Serie B and Serie C! Palmeiras 878 matches total? Palmeiras 2012-2013 in Serie B: +76 matches. Serie A 2003-2023 for Palmeiras (minus 2012-13 in B) ≈ 19 seasons × 38 ≈ 722 + Copa ~70 + Libertadores ~80 ≈ 870-880 ✓. OK plausible.\n\nSo current state is likely correct. Earlier \"387\" — probably from the state BEFORE the bare-alias fix when Flamengo/São Paulo were SPLIT entities (São Paulo 387 as the bare \"sao paulo\" entity — no wait...). Whatever — the question is whether the CURRENT numbers are RIGHT. The merge quality is validated by champions/h2h/derbies. Away points ranking: Flamengo 486 (950 matches) — each away match can give 3 pts; 475 away matches × ~1.02. Plausible ✓.\n\nHmm, but actually — let me double-check against a quick manual computation of Flamengo away points from raw Brasileirao file only to make sure the aggregation isn't inflated by duplicates... The curated list has no exact dups (tested). 2019 Serie A = 380 single-source ✓. OK I'm fairly confident.\n\nSo: fix the test to assert the CURRENT, verified behavior: Flamengo or Cruzeiro top away_points with 486; better — assert structural: rankings sorted desc; top team away_points == max. And assert Flamengo's away_points == 486? That's data-derived... I verified it. But a more robust test: `ranking[\"rankings\"][0][\"away_points\"] >= ranking[\"rankings\"][1][\"away_points\"]` + top3 teams known set {Flamengo, Cruzeiro, Palmeiras}? I'll assert Flamengo is first (data-verified) — hmm, actually let me make it more future-proof: assert rankings[0][\"away_points\"] == 486 AND team in {\"Flamengo\", \"Cruzeiro\"} (tie order depends on secondary sort). Cleaner: assert the top team's away_points == 486 and set of top-3 teams == {Flamengo, Cruzeiro, Palmeiras}. Hmm — the tiebreak order: sorted by (metric, points, goal_diff) — Flamengo 486 vs Cruzeiro 486 — points: Flamengo total points? Cruzeiro total? Whichever. Test: `ranking[\"rankings\"][0][\"team\"] in {\"Flamengo\", \"Cruzeiro\"}` and top away_points == 486. Plus most_wins_in_2019 etc. already fine.\n\nActually — simpler and more meaningful: keep the away-record test but assert against RECOMPUTED truth from the repo itself (compute expected away points for the top team directly from matches — that's circular). Hmm. Best: assert away_points of Flamengo == 486 AND Cruzeiro == 486 (both verified data points), plus ordering invariants. Fine.\n\nAlso — the standings for \"most goals 2019\" (Flamengo 86 ✓ verified earlier).\n\n5. **Grenal/derbies 2019**: entity keys — fix DERBIES to resolve via _resolve_one at query time. Also \"test_derbies_2019_include_named_rivalries\" failed — probably Grenal/Fla-Flu missing (Fla-Flu key \"flamengo\" doesn't exist — it's \"flamengo rj\"!). The current derby_matches: DERBIES has (\"flamengo\",\"fluminense\") etc. — broken keys → only derbies with valid collapsed keys matched (Clássico-Rei ceara/fortaleza ✓, Clássico Mineiro atletico mg/cruzeiro ✓, Dérbi Campineiro ponte preta/guarani sp ✓, Derby Paulista palmeiras/corinthians ✓, Choque-Rei palmeiras/sao paulo ✓). Grenal broken (internacional rs), Fla-Flu broken (flamengo rj). Derbies 2023=16 (without Grenal/Fla-Flu!). After fix the counts will CHANGE: derbies 2023 and 2019 will grow. I'll recompute expected numbers after the fix and update tests.\n\n6. **find_team Atletico disambiguation**: remove bare \"atletico\" alias (keep \"athletico\"). Then resolve(\"Atletico\") → (\"atletico\", None) → base \"atletico\" not an entity (no bare) → same_base → 3 entities ✓. BUT — WAIT: does any DATA row have bare \"Atletico\"?? The Libertadores \"Athletico\" (with h) → alias \"athletico\" → \"atletico pr\" ✓ handled by the \"athletico\" key. Is there bare \"Atletico\" (no h) anywhere in data? BR-Football? Earlier counter: BR-Football bare big-club names didn't include 'atletico' (0). Libertadores bare: \"Athletico\" 29 (with h) ✓. So no bare \"Atletico\" in data ✓ safe to drop the alias.\n\n   Hmm — but one more: \"Atletico\" alias removal — then split_team(\"Atletico\") → (\"atletico\", None) — used in resolve only. ✓\n\n7. **test_state_suffix_and_plain_spellings / accented spellings**: add big-club bare aliases (palmeiras→palmeiras sp, gremio→gremio rs, etc.) so bare and suffixed pairs are IDENTICAL pairs. Let me add the full set.\n\n8. **test_player_detail_by_fifa_id**: Messi id 158023 → \"L. Messi\" ✓ fix test.\n\n9. **test_forwards_from_a_club**: Santos has exactly 3 forwards → change assertion to >= 3 and check first 3. ✓\n\n10. **test_unknown_team**: \"Bora Bora United\" ✓.\n\nAlso — the DERBIES fix: resolve at query time with _resolve_one. Note \"Vitória\" in Ba-Vi → alias \"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\" ✓ entity \"vitoria ba\" ✓ exists (quals {ba, es}). ✓. \"Botafogo\" (Clássico Vovô) → alias → \"botafogo rj\" ✓. \"Santos\" → \"santos sp\" ✓. \"Internacional\" → \"internacional rs\" ✓.\n\nLet me now apply all fixes:\n\nA. normalize.py:\n   - remove \"atletico\": \"atletico pr\" (keep \"athletico\")\n   - add bare big-club aliases: palmeiras, corinthians, sao paulo, gremio, cruzeiro, vasco, bahia, ceara, fortaleza, chapecoense, coritiba, criciuma, figueirense, avai, goias, sport, parana, ponte preta, joinville, csa, cuiaba\n\n   WAIT — careful with \"sport\" → \"sport pe\": there's ALSO \"sport recife\" alias ✓ consistent. And bare \"Sport\" from novo → (\"sport